1
00:00:05,100 --> 00:00:08,267
♪ In the not
Too distant future ♪

2
00:00:08,267 --> 00:00:10,367
♪ Next Sunday A.D. ♪

3
00:00:11,567 --> 00:00:14,467
♪ There was a guy named Joel ♪

4
00:00:14,467 --> 00:00:17,000
♪ Not too different
From you or me ♪

5
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:20,800
♪ He worked
At Gizmonic Institute ♪

6
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:23,700
♪ Just another face
In a red jumpsuit ♪

7
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:26,800
♪ He did a good job
Cleaning up the place ♪

8
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:30,733
♪ But his boss didn't like him
So they shot him into space ♪

9
00:00:33,300 --> 00:00:36,200
♪ We'll send him cheesy movies ♪

10
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:39,667
♪ The worst we can find
La, la, la ♪

11
00:00:39,667 --> 00:00:42,533
♪ He'll have to sit
and watch them all ♪

12
00:00:42,533 --> 00:00:45,033
♪ And we'll monitor his mind ♪

13
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:48,600
♪ Now keep in mind
Joel can't control ♪

14
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:51,300
♪ Where the movies begin
Or end ♪

15
00:00:52,067 --> 00:00:55,100
♪ Because he used
Those special parts ♪

16
00:00:55,100 --> 00:00:58,000
♪ To make his robot friends ♪

17
00:01:00,100 --> 00:01:01,867
♪ Robot roll call ♪

18
00:01:01,867 --> 00:01:03,167
♪ Cam-bot ♪

19
00:01:03,167 --> 00:01:04,467
-♪ Gypsy ♪
-Hi, girl.

20
00:01:04,467 --> 00:01:06,267
-♪ Tom Servo ♪
-All good.

21
00:01:06,267 --> 00:01:07,833
♪ Crow! ♪

22
00:01:07,833 --> 00:01:10,700
♪ If you're wondering
How he eats and breathes ♪

23
00:01:10,700 --> 00:01:13,667
♪ And other science facts
La, la, la ♪

24
00:01:13,667 --> 00:01:16,833
♪ Then repeat to yourself
It's just a show ♪

25
00:01:16,833 --> 00:01:20,033
♪ I should really just relax ♪

26
00:01:20,033 --> 00:01:25,233
♪ For Mystery
Science Theater 3000 ♪

27
00:01:35,867 --> 00:01:40,000
All right now, bring it down.
Bring it down and back up again.

28
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:41,333
Back up.

29
00:01:41,333 --> 00:01:44,067
Good. Hey, Cam-bot,
bring it in a little bit. Wow.

30
00:01:44,067 --> 00:01:46,167
[Intercom] Thirty seconds
to commercial sign.

31
00:01:46,167 --> 00:01:48,333
Wow, well it's really good
to see you again.

32
00:01:48,333 --> 00:01:50,700
As you can see, we've done a lot
to change the ship

33
00:01:50,700 --> 00:01:52,533
and now we're working on
Tom Servo's voice

34
00:01:52,533 --> 00:01:54,367
so there's
really a lot going on.

35
00:01:54,367 --> 00:01:57,667
And I have a terrible toothache.
Ow!

36
00:01:57,667 --> 00:01:59,100
Uh, he's a robot.

37
00:01:59,100 --> 00:02:00,867
He doesn't have any teeth,
but don't tell him.

38
00:02:00,867 --> 00:02:02,133
Huh? What?

39
00:02:02,133 --> 00:02:04,300
[Intercom]
Commercial sign in ten seconds.

40
00:02:04,300 --> 00:02:07,100
Anyway, I'm really glad
you're here, so stick around.

41
00:02:07,567 --> 00:02:09,000
[Intercom]
Commercial sign in five,

42
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:11,600
four, three, two, one.

43
00:02:11,600 --> 00:02:12,933
Commercial sign out.

44
00:02:13,933 --> 00:02:15,000
See ya.

45
00:02:16,133 --> 00:02:19,333
[theme music playing]

46
00:02:21,433 --> 00:02:24,300
Uh, don't use Novocain.
It dulls the senses.

47
00:02:24,300 --> 00:02:26,000
-Okay, get ready. Open.
-Mm-mm.

48
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:29,600
-Let's go. Come on, open it up.
-Okay. Ahh...

49
00:02:30,067 --> 00:02:31,567
-[drilling]
-Ah, ah, ah, ah!

50
00:02:31,567 --> 00:02:34,333
That's okay. I got it. Here.
Okay.

51
00:02:34,333 --> 00:02:36,333
-[drilling]
-Ah, ah, ah!

52
00:02:36,333 --> 00:02:38,900
Oh, we're back. Hey, Cam-bot,
push it back a little bit.

53
00:02:38,900 --> 00:02:40,667
So much has been going--
you can let go of him.

54
00:02:40,667 --> 00:02:42,800
So much has been
going on on the ship,

55
00:02:42,800 --> 00:02:45,433
but the biggest thing
is Tom Servo's new voice.

56
00:02:45,433 --> 00:02:46,900
We're just about
to show it to you.

57
00:02:47,467 --> 00:02:48,633
[drilling]

58
00:02:48,633 --> 00:02:50,500
All right, give her a rip, Tom.
Let's see how it sounds.

59
00:02:50,500 --> 00:02:53,200
Warning, danger, danger,
Will Robinson. Warning.

60
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:54,867
Wait, hold, hold, hold it.
I don't think--

61
00:02:54,867 --> 00:02:56,867
I don't think
it's quite there yet, okay.

62
00:02:58,533 --> 00:03:00,067
[drilling]

63
00:03:00,067 --> 00:03:01,567
Okay, there. Give it a shot.

64
00:03:01,567 --> 00:03:05,900
[Tom] [singing]
♪ Daisy, Daisy ♪

65
00:03:05,900 --> 00:03:07,467
-♪ Give me your... ♪
-No, that's not quite it.

66
00:03:07,467 --> 00:03:08,667
Okay. Here we got it.

67
00:03:08,667 --> 00:03:09,900
[drilling]

68
00:03:09,900 --> 00:03:12,133
There we go. That-- Here it is.

69
00:03:12,133 --> 00:03:15,200
Hello, world. Ha!
Happy birthday! Ha ha ha!

70
00:03:15,200 --> 00:03:17,267
What do you think?
Pretty neat, huh?

71
00:03:17,267 --> 00:03:18,867
Oh, it's a world of difference.

72
00:03:18,867 --> 00:03:22,967
You know, Joel, I'm just finally
realizing I've been to paradise,

73
00:03:22,967 --> 00:03:25,000
but I've never been to me.
[laughing]

74
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:28,100
Oh, look sharp you goons.
It's the dark overlord.

75
00:03:28,733 --> 00:03:30,700
Hi. Welcome to Deep 13.

76
00:03:30,700 --> 00:03:33,600
Would you like to try our
smooth, creamy thruster buster?

77
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:36,667
Where's Dr. Earhart
and Dr. Forrester?

78
00:03:36,667 --> 00:03:38,533
I'm Frank. I'm new here.

79
00:03:38,533 --> 00:03:41,467
As for Dr. Forrester,
he stepped out for a moment.

80
00:03:41,467 --> 00:03:43,200
As for Dr. Earhart...

81
00:03:43,833 --> 00:03:45,233
he's missing.

82
00:03:45,233 --> 00:03:46,733
[Everybody] Oh.

83
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:49,467
But as far as you
space monkeys are concerned,

84
00:03:49,467 --> 00:03:51,500
I'm the new marshal in town.
That's right.

85
00:03:51,500 --> 00:03:53,933
I'm the God. I'm the God!

86
00:03:55,233 --> 00:03:56,667
May I take your order, please.

87
00:03:56,667 --> 00:03:57,833
[chuckling]

88
00:03:57,833 --> 00:04:00,400
Yeah,
what's in a thruster buster?

89
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:04,067
Uh, thruster buster is a thick,

90
00:04:04,067 --> 00:04:09,133
four-pound slice of our
oven tempered meal food,

91
00:04:09,133 --> 00:04:13,500
covered with wet runny
blister of growth compost,

92
00:04:13,500 --> 00:04:16,633
onions, lettuce,
mayonnaise, tomatoes,

93
00:04:16,633 --> 00:04:19,000
special sauce,
skanked while you sleep.

94
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:20,467
Would you like
some fries with that?

95
00:04:20,467 --> 00:04:21,633
Uh, no.

96
00:04:21,633 --> 00:04:26,233
We'll have,
uh, four solar burgers

97
00:04:26,233 --> 00:04:30,000
and a four space buds.

98
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:31,967
Uh, would you like something
to drink with that?

99
00:04:31,967 --> 00:04:35,500
Uh, yeah, uh,
what do you guys want to drink?

100
00:04:35,500 --> 00:04:38,000
Uh, I'll have a splunky, mango.

101
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:40,033
Uh, cream splunky for me,
please.

102
00:04:40,033 --> 00:04:41,233
Mix.

103
00:04:41,233 --> 00:04:43,100
Okay, I'll have a mango, too.

104
00:04:43,100 --> 00:04:45,433
Okay,
so that's four solar burgers,

105
00:04:45,433 --> 00:04:48,900
four space buds,
uh, four splunkies,

106
00:04:48,900 --> 00:04:52,300
two mango,
one mix and one cream.

107
00:04:52,300 --> 00:04:54,100
Uh, are you going
to be eating out

108
00:04:54,100 --> 00:04:55,800
or will you be
dining in with us?

109
00:04:55,800 --> 00:04:58,533
Uh, what the heck?
We'll eat in.

110
00:04:58,533 --> 00:05:00,067
Bring us down to Earth.

111
00:05:00,067 --> 00:05:02,333
That's the button
on the left over there.

112
00:05:02,933 --> 00:05:05,867
Got it. Oh, Jerry, listen,
I need you right away.

113
00:05:05,867 --> 00:05:08,300
Set up four...
a table for four, okay?

114
00:05:08,300 --> 00:05:09,433
Thanks, a lot.

115
00:05:09,433 --> 00:05:12,867
Uh, Sylvia, I need you to take
these thruster busters

116
00:05:12,867 --> 00:05:14,733
to this address right here.

117
00:05:14,733 --> 00:05:17,600
Listen, no burrowing up through
the bedrooms this time, okay?

118
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:18,667
Thanks.

119
00:05:18,667 --> 00:05:21,167
How's our new enterprise going?

120
00:05:21,167 --> 00:05:22,833
Sold another one.

121
00:05:23,067 --> 00:05:24,333
-Oh.
-That's right.

122
00:05:24,333 --> 00:05:27,667
Uh, just sold our first order
to dine in as a matter of fact.

123
00:05:27,667 --> 00:05:29,667
Uh, Frank. Frank, look at me
when I'm talking to you.

124
00:05:29,667 --> 00:05:31,233
We don't do dine in.

125
00:05:31,233 --> 00:05:32,833
We only do take out.

126
00:05:32,833 --> 00:05:34,333
Who wants to dine in anyway?

127
00:05:34,333 --> 00:05:35,367
Oh, it's, uh...

128
00:05:35,933 --> 00:05:37,400
it's Joel and the robots.

129
00:05:37,400 --> 00:05:39,200
I was just about
to bring them down.

130
00:05:39,200 --> 00:05:40,233
What? No!

131
00:05:40,433 --> 00:05:44,267
Uh-oh. Looks like the splunky's
about to hit the fan.

132
00:05:44,267 --> 00:05:48,433
-There.
-Oh! Owie! Owie! Owie! Owie!

133
00:05:48,433 --> 00:05:51,633
Frank, go get this
week's invention exchange.

134
00:05:52,600 --> 00:05:54,967
And as for you,
my takeout troglodyte,

135
00:05:54,967 --> 00:05:57,900
the only thing you're going
to be served is more bad movies.

136
00:05:57,900 --> 00:05:59,733
Now I know Frank isn't much,

137
00:05:59,733 --> 00:06:02,500
but he's showing a lot of
promise in the invention area,

138
00:06:02,500 --> 00:06:04,867
and I don't think you're going
to be able to put one over on us

139
00:06:04,867 --> 00:06:07,967
this week,
but you go ahead, Joel.

140
00:06:07,967 --> 00:06:10,533
Hit me with your best shot.
Fire away.

141
00:06:10,867 --> 00:06:13,100
Hey, sirs, what do you think?

142
00:06:13,100 --> 00:06:15,700
It's my new invention.
Back it up, Cam-bot.

143
00:06:15,700 --> 00:06:18,200
It's called the BGC19.

144
00:06:18,200 --> 00:06:21,067
And it's based on the premise
that drummers just don't get

145
00:06:21,067 --> 00:06:22,967
enough attention
way in the back.

146
00:06:22,967 --> 00:06:26,700
With the BGC19, you can supply
that all-important backbeat.

147
00:06:26,700 --> 00:06:28,767
[drums playing]

148
00:06:32,500 --> 00:06:34,933
And still walk around
and be a dynamic front man

149
00:06:34,933 --> 00:06:36,333
at the same time.

150
00:06:36,333 --> 00:06:38,033
Now what's your invention?

151
00:06:38,267 --> 00:06:39,500
That's it. That's it.

152
00:06:39,500 --> 00:06:41,467
That's good.
That's right. Thanks.

153
00:06:41,467 --> 00:06:44,567
Well, it's called the BGC19.

154
00:06:44,567 --> 00:06:47,467
It's based on the premise that
most drummers being in the back

155
00:06:47,467 --> 00:06:48,533
don't get enough attention.

156
00:06:48,533 --> 00:06:50,033
-Frank.
-Hmm?

157
00:06:52,400 --> 00:06:54,667
-There!
-Oh! Owie! Owie! Owie!

158
00:06:54,667 --> 00:06:56,200
I didn't know
it's wrong to steal!

159
00:06:56,200 --> 00:06:58,467
Who are you to judge?

160
00:06:58,767 --> 00:07:00,567
Well, Joel,
under the circumstances,

161
00:07:00,567 --> 00:07:03,733
I'm afraid I'm going to have
to stick it to you this week.

162
00:07:03,733 --> 00:07:09,033
Rocketship X-M will assault
your pop and folk sensibilities.

163
00:07:09,033 --> 00:07:13,400
It's a chillingly uninteresting
assault of mind-numbing,

164
00:07:13,400 --> 00:07:16,533
gut-wrenching,
brain-bloating non-action.

165
00:07:16,533 --> 00:07:19,467
Oh, Lloyd Bridges movie?
I guess I could handle that.

166
00:07:19,467 --> 00:07:23,167
Uh, Lloyd Bridges
with Hugh O'Brian?

167
00:07:23,700 --> 00:07:27,300
-[yells] No! And movie siren!
-[alarm blaring]

168
00:07:36,233 --> 00:07:39,400
[music playing]

169
00:07:39,400 --> 00:07:40,933
[Tom]
Robert L. Lippert presents...

170
00:07:40,933 --> 00:07:42,700
[Joel] Hey, I showed
them the BGC19.

171
00:07:42,700 --> 00:07:44,100
I think they really liked it.

172
00:07:44,100 --> 00:07:45,833
-[Crow] Cool.
-[Joel] There's a new guy...

173
00:07:45,833 --> 00:07:47,633
that new guy stole it.

174
00:07:47,833 --> 00:07:49,433
-[Tom] Oh, look.
-[Crow] Lloyd Bridges?

175
00:07:49,433 --> 00:07:51,833
By this time, his lungs
would be aching for air.

176
00:07:51,833 --> 00:07:53,900
[Tom and Crow laugh]

177
00:07:54,533 --> 00:07:56,033
[Joel] Hey, "Huge" O'Brian.

178
00:07:56,033 --> 00:07:58,500
[Tom humming music]

179
00:08:02,767 --> 00:08:04,800
[Tom] Ready? Here we go.

180
00:08:07,333 --> 00:08:11,367
[all singing] ♪ Don't get
Your hopes up too high ♪

181
00:08:11,367 --> 00:08:13,567
♪ Because when it's
All said and done ♪

182
00:08:13,567 --> 00:08:16,467
♪ It's a Lloyd Bridges movie ♪

183
00:08:16,467 --> 00:08:20,067
♪ If you're looking
for something good ♪

184
00:08:20,067 --> 00:08:24,633
♪ Then an hour from now,
you're going to be weeping ♪

185
00:08:24,900 --> 00:08:26,567
[music continues playing]

186
00:08:28,500 --> 00:08:30,167
[Joel] Communism
by Clarence Marks.

187
00:08:30,167 --> 00:08:31,567
[Tom] Ice cream by Frank Heath.

188
00:08:31,567 --> 00:08:33,233
[Crow] Gas by Betty Sinclair.

189
00:08:33,233 --> 00:08:34,633
[Tom] Cowboy hats
by Clarence Steensen.

190
00:08:34,633 --> 00:08:35,833
[Joel] Financed by Don Cash.

191
00:08:35,833 --> 00:08:37,233
[Crow] Vibes by Orville Hampton.

192
00:08:37,233 --> 00:08:38,667
[Joel] Chapstick by Mary Chaffee

193
00:08:38,667 --> 00:08:41,433
[Crow] Stupid name
by Ferde Grofe guy.

194
00:08:42,067 --> 00:08:44,867
[Tom] Oh, Murray,
the ceiling needs painting.

195
00:08:45,867 --> 00:08:47,800
[music continues playing]

196
00:08:50,233 --> 00:08:51,667
[Tom] Kind of cheery, isn't it?

197
00:08:54,567 --> 00:08:55,600
[Tom] Hmm.

198
00:09:01,233 --> 00:09:03,667
[Joel] Well, that guy's
trespassing right there.

199
00:09:03,667 --> 00:09:04,967
[Crow] Hmm.

200
00:09:04,967 --> 00:09:07,933
[Joel] Yeah, what's he
trying to prove anyway?

201
00:09:07,933 --> 00:09:09,233
Smart guy.

202
00:09:09,233 --> 00:09:10,833
[Tom] Let him carry watches.

203
00:09:14,767 --> 00:09:16,400
[Crow] Hey, Petey, I'll race ya.

204
00:09:16,400 --> 00:09:18,033
[car sounds]

205
00:09:18,600 --> 00:09:20,400
[Joel] Marry me, Steve.

206
00:09:21,933 --> 00:09:24,933
[Crow] Well, your lifeline shows
you'll meet four interesting...

207
00:09:24,933 --> 00:09:27,233
[Tom] Now wrap it
around her neck.

208
00:09:29,933 --> 00:09:31,467
[Joel] Either this man is dead

209
00:09:31,467 --> 00:09:33,400
or my watch has stopped.
[chuckles]

210
00:09:36,700 --> 00:09:39,700
[Crow] So not as good as us,
is he? [chuckles]

211
00:09:40,667 --> 00:09:42,367
Nothing to be alarmed about.

212
00:09:42,367 --> 00:09:44,867
Quite understandable
under the circumstance.

213
00:09:44,867 --> 00:09:46,000
The weaker sex.

214
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:47,933
The only one whose
blood pressure is normal.

215
00:09:47,933 --> 00:09:49,000
[Joel] Good one, Lloyd.

216
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:50,933
[PA] X minus 17 minutes.

217
00:09:51,500 --> 00:09:53,333
We're due in the assembly hall
in another minute.

218
00:09:53,333 --> 00:09:55,767
[Crow] Assembly today?
That means no class. Hurray.

219
00:09:55,767 --> 00:09:57,467
Thank you, doctor,
and good night.

220
00:10:00,400 --> 00:10:01,800
[Joel] So who's for ribs?

221
00:10:01,800 --> 00:10:04,467
[Tom] Going down to Rudy's?
I'm going to the market, maybe.

222
00:10:04,467 --> 00:10:06,100
Get some onion rings.
Want a full loaf?

223
00:10:06,100 --> 00:10:07,100
I think I can handle it.

224
00:10:07,100 --> 00:10:08,567
[Joel] I'm going to get some
for my wife.

225
00:10:08,567 --> 00:10:09,667
[Tom] I'm gonna phone ahead.

226
00:10:09,667 --> 00:10:11,867
-[chatter]
-Good stuff. I like it.

227
00:10:13,400 --> 00:10:15,667
-I ordered some for my wife.
-[Crow] Rhubarb, rhubarb.

228
00:10:17,067 --> 00:10:18,500
Scientific rhubarb.

229
00:10:21,533 --> 00:10:23,833
[Crow] And now, the cast of
I Married Four Astronauts.

230
00:10:24,133 --> 00:10:26,267
[Joel] You know,
we think we can take 'em.

231
00:10:27,900 --> 00:10:29,933
[PA] X minus 16 minutes.

232
00:10:31,700 --> 00:10:34,600
[Tom] In a few minutes, I'll be
cutting these people open.

233
00:10:35,567 --> 00:10:37,700
[Crow] Our panel tonight
will be Robert Q. Lewis,

234
00:10:37,700 --> 00:10:41,033
Kitty Carlisle, Arleen Francis
and Dorothy Kil...

235
00:10:41,033 --> 00:10:44,067
Uh, no, I'm sorry. Dorothy
won't be appearing tonight.

236
00:10:45,800 --> 00:10:46,867
Ladies and gentlemen.

237
00:10:46,867 --> 00:10:48,600
[Joel] Our first structure
is called tag.

238
00:10:48,600 --> 00:10:51,767
Every news service and newspaper
is represented here tonight.

239
00:10:51,767 --> 00:10:53,200
[Tom]
All 12 newspapers in the world.

240
00:10:53,200 --> 00:10:56,967
For your cooperation in the past
when complete secrecy was vital,

241
00:10:56,967 --> 00:10:58,033
we are grateful.

242
00:10:58,033 --> 00:10:59,733
[Crow]
Thank you for being gutless.

243
00:10:59,733 --> 00:11:01,733
However, I must make
something clear.

244
00:11:01,733 --> 00:11:03,000
[Tom] I'm wearing a bra.

245
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:06,600
Although ours is not strictly
a military project,

246
00:11:07,133 --> 00:11:09,400
a great deal of our research
and knowledge

247
00:11:09,400 --> 00:11:11,733
falls within
security regulations.

248
00:11:12,300 --> 00:11:14,000
Therefore, I must insist

249
00:11:14,300 --> 00:11:16,467
that you reveal
only such information

250
00:11:16,467 --> 00:11:19,400
as appears
in the authorized press release,

251
00:11:19,833 --> 00:11:21,633
which will be
handed to you later.

252
00:11:21,633 --> 00:11:23,600
[Joel] Young Republicans are
in the back handing them out.

253
00:11:23,600 --> 00:11:25,567
I'm sure that we can
all recall the wild tales

254
00:11:25,567 --> 00:11:28,600
of the flying disks,
flying saucers, space ships.

255
00:11:28,600 --> 00:11:29,833
[Joel] Frisbees,
weather balloons...

256
00:11:29,833 --> 00:11:31,267
And who knows
what imaginative creations.

257
00:11:31,633 --> 00:11:34,933
The press is to be commended for
discounting in most instances

258
00:11:34,933 --> 00:11:36,733
these premature rumors.

259
00:11:36,733 --> 00:11:39,767
And so helping to minimize
public apprehension.

260
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:44,100
Tonight, you are invited here
to witness an important event.

261
00:11:44,100 --> 00:11:45,267
[Joel] My outing.

262
00:11:45,267 --> 00:11:48,067
You are all familiar with
our previous work in sending

263
00:11:48,067 --> 00:11:50,200
robot missiles into space.

264
00:11:50,800 --> 00:11:52,567
That phase is at an end.

265
00:11:52,567 --> 00:11:54,167
[Crow] I killed it,
and I'm glad.

266
00:11:54,400 --> 00:11:58,633
Tonight we will launch
the first manned space ship.

267
00:11:58,633 --> 00:12:00,200
[Tom] Then we love.

268
00:12:00,200 --> 00:12:02,100
The RXM.

269
00:12:02,667 --> 00:12:05,233
Rocketship Expedition Moon.

270
00:12:05,700 --> 00:12:07,033
[PA] X minus 13 minutes.

271
00:12:07,033 --> 00:12:08,233
[Joel]
Hey, do you have a pencil?

272
00:12:08,233 --> 00:12:10,033
[Crow]
Does expedition start with an x?

273
00:12:10,033 --> 00:12:13,267
...dreamed of visiting
the nearest of heavenly bodies.

274
00:12:13,967 --> 00:12:16,967
Some for adventurous
fantastic reasons,

275
00:12:17,767 --> 00:12:21,333
others like ourselves because
they... visualize

276
00:12:21,667 --> 00:12:23,100
the successful lunar expedition.

277
00:12:23,100 --> 00:12:24,633
[Crow] Others to get chicks.

278
00:12:24,633 --> 00:12:27,967
As the first step toward
practical interplanetary travel.

279
00:12:29,500 --> 00:12:31,467
Today there is even
the possibility

280
00:12:32,267 --> 00:12:35,333
that an unassailable base
could be established on the moon

281
00:12:35,767 --> 00:12:37,267
to control world peace.

282
00:12:37,267 --> 00:12:38,933
[Joel] Our kind of world peace.

283
00:12:39,367 --> 00:12:40,700
[Fleming] I will now
introduce to you

284
00:12:40,700 --> 00:12:43,100
the head of this expedition
and his crew.

285
00:12:43,100 --> 00:12:44,633
[Tom] He's a widower who enjoys

286
00:12:44,633 --> 00:12:46,467
long walks on the beach
and parasailing.

287
00:12:46,467 --> 00:12:49,333
[Fleming] Designer of the RXM,
and as you all know,

288
00:12:49,333 --> 00:12:51,933
one of the most brilliant
physicists of the day

289
00:12:52,400 --> 00:12:53,800
and an old friend.

290
00:12:54,467 --> 00:12:56,633
-Dr. Lisa Van Horn...
-[Tom] Rooty toot toot.

291
00:12:56,633 --> 00:12:59,300
[Fleming] ...his most abled
coworker and assistant,

292
00:12:59,300 --> 00:13:00,600
doctor of chemistry...

293
00:13:00,600 --> 00:13:01,900
[all] And a good friend.

294
00:13:01,900 --> 00:13:04,600
[Fleming] Floyd Graham, pilot.

295
00:13:04,600 --> 00:13:05,867
[all] And an old friend.

296
00:13:05,867 --> 00:13:09,100
[Fleming] Mr. Harry Chamberlain,
astronomer of the Mount Wilson

297
00:13:09,100 --> 00:13:10,467
and Palomar Observatory.

298
00:13:10,467 --> 00:13:11,567
[Joel] Dirk Square jaw.

299
00:13:11,567 --> 00:13:13,867
[Fleming] Mr. Chamberlain
will serve as navigator.

300
00:13:13,867 --> 00:13:14,933
[Crow] I never liked him.

301
00:13:14,933 --> 00:13:17,300
[Fleming] Major
William Corrigan, engineer.

302
00:13:17,300 --> 00:13:18,367
[Tom] Comic relief.

303
00:13:18,367 --> 00:13:20,033
[Fleming] I shall now
ask Dr. Eckstrom

304
00:13:20,033 --> 00:13:21,967
to outline
the flight plan for you.

305
00:13:22,433 --> 00:13:24,567
-Carl?
-[Crow clapping]

306
00:13:24,567 --> 00:13:26,333
[Crow] Uh, thank you darling...
I mean, Larry.

307
00:13:26,333 --> 00:13:27,967
[PA] X minus 14 minutes.

308
00:13:29,567 --> 00:13:30,633
[Tom] Oh, no hurry.

309
00:13:30,633 --> 00:13:33,467
[Joel] Now Rommel
will be at base camp here.

310
00:13:33,667 --> 00:13:35,433
The distance between
the Earth and the moon

311
00:13:35,433 --> 00:13:39,167
at its closest proximity
is 238,000 miles.

312
00:13:39,167 --> 00:13:40,367
[Joel] Neat, huh?

313
00:13:40,367 --> 00:13:43,667
We expect to cover this distance
in approximately 48 hours.

314
00:13:43,667 --> 00:13:45,933
[Crow] New York Times
spit your gum out.

315
00:13:45,933 --> 00:13:47,133
...will be the ascent

316
00:13:47,867 --> 00:13:49,933
to an altitude of 300 miles.

317
00:13:50,233 --> 00:13:52,433
From the start while we
pass through the troposphere

318
00:13:52,433 --> 00:13:55,067
and stratosphere
until we reach the ionosphere,

319
00:13:55,067 --> 00:13:57,533
the flight will be controlled
by our automatic pilot.

320
00:13:57,533 --> 00:13:58,767
[Joel] Named Timmy.

321
00:13:58,767 --> 00:14:00,700
[Dr. Eckstrom] After we have
climbed through the atmosphere,

322
00:14:00,700 --> 00:14:02,200
we will turn the rocket

323
00:14:02,533 --> 00:14:05,833
into a parallel course
with the surface of the Earth.

324
00:14:07,933 --> 00:14:10,533
In flying parallel
with the Earth's surface,

325
00:14:10,533 --> 00:14:13,267
we receive added boosts
from its rotation.

326
00:14:13,267 --> 00:14:16,667
[Crow] And we'll be collecting
bonus coins and mushrooms...

327
00:14:16,667 --> 00:14:17,800
[PA] X minus 13 minutes.

328
00:14:17,800 --> 00:14:20,733
[Tom] We baked a rocket earlier
in a 450-degree oven.

329
00:14:20,733 --> 00:14:24,000
The RXM is what is known as
a multistage or step rocket.

330
00:14:24,333 --> 00:14:25,533
[Joel] At this point,

331
00:14:25,533 --> 00:14:28,667
the rocket becomes engorged
with astronauts.

332
00:14:28,667 --> 00:14:30,333
...will be jettison.

333
00:14:30,900 --> 00:14:33,333
The nose section, which is
a complete rocket containing

334
00:14:33,333 --> 00:14:37,233
enough fuel for the entire trip
and also containing our cabin,

335
00:14:37,433 --> 00:14:39,867
overcomes the gravitational pull
of the Earth

336
00:14:39,867 --> 00:14:41,500
and heads in the direction
of the moon.

337
00:14:41,833 --> 00:14:43,167
[Joel] Then we stop
for a quick snack.

338
00:14:43,167 --> 00:14:44,667
After we have passed
the equilibrium point,

339
00:14:44,667 --> 00:14:47,500
where the Earth and the moon's
gravities are in balance,

340
00:14:48,100 --> 00:14:50,067
we will reduce power
to a minimum.

341
00:14:50,467 --> 00:14:53,200
The moon's attraction will
carry us the rest of the way.

342
00:14:53,200 --> 00:14:55,500
[Tom] With the grace of God
and a long-handled spoon.

343
00:14:55,500 --> 00:14:58,267
Finally, we will reverse
the rocket,

344
00:14:58,267 --> 00:15:01,433
utilizing the thrust of its
motors to make our landing.

345
00:15:01,433 --> 00:15:03,533
[Crow] Uh, could you repeat
that part about the moon?

346
00:15:03,900 --> 00:15:06,600
A few more details
which might interest you.

347
00:15:07,500 --> 00:15:10,300
The cabin is pressurized
and gyro controlled

348
00:15:10,300 --> 00:15:13,000
keeping us in a level position
at all times.

349
00:15:13,567 --> 00:15:17,567
We carry radar for navigation,
short wave for communication.

350
00:15:17,567 --> 00:15:19,700
[Joel] Eight tracks
and cassettes in stereo.

351
00:15:19,700 --> 00:15:21,933
...more than twice the amount
of fuel we expect to use

352
00:15:21,933 --> 00:15:23,833
to keep well within
the margin of safety.

353
00:15:23,833 --> 00:15:25,067
[Crow] Like most
American vehicles.

354
00:15:25,067 --> 00:15:26,367
[PA] X minus 12 minutes.

355
00:15:26,367 --> 00:15:27,433
[Tom] Thank you, God.

356
00:15:27,433 --> 00:15:28,467
We still have a few moments,

357
00:15:28,467 --> 00:15:29,633
ladies and gentlemen,
for questions

358
00:15:29,633 --> 00:15:30,967
if any of you care to...

359
00:15:30,967 --> 00:15:33,100
[Tom] The laser breast exam
is on Friday,

360
00:15:33,100 --> 00:15:34,567
and remember
I'll be in my office Tuesday,

361
00:15:34,567 --> 00:15:35,800
but not Wednesday.

362
00:15:37,133 --> 00:15:39,233
[theme music playing]

363
00:15:43,400 --> 00:15:44,733
Chamberlain...

364
00:15:46,033 --> 00:15:47,667
how do you fit into the picture?

365
00:15:47,667 --> 00:15:49,767
Do you...
do you have any family?

366
00:15:49,767 --> 00:15:51,067
[Crow] Not that I know of.

367
00:15:51,067 --> 00:15:52,567
I've been living
on mountaintops.

368
00:15:52,567 --> 00:15:55,067
I'm afraid it's too lonely
for such opportunities.

369
00:15:55,467 --> 00:15:57,967
-Have you ever done any flying?
-[Joel] Oh, yeah.

370
00:15:57,967 --> 00:15:59,867
But you're the navigator.

371
00:16:00,267 --> 00:16:03,333
You must realize that intersolar
flight requires far more exact

372
00:16:03,333 --> 00:16:05,933
and precise navigation
than any earthbound voyage.

373
00:16:05,933 --> 00:16:07,767
-Um-hmm.
-Plus that's incidental.

374
00:16:07,767 --> 00:16:09,567
-It is?
-Yes.

375
00:16:09,933 --> 00:16:11,100
You see, as an astronomer,

376
00:16:11,100 --> 00:16:13,300
I can appreciate the perfect
observation conditions

377
00:16:13,300 --> 00:16:14,600
on a body without atmosphere.

378
00:16:14,600 --> 00:16:16,233
-[Tom] And you can't.
-Such is our moon.

379
00:16:16,433 --> 00:16:17,733
Do you realize
that within one hour...

380
00:16:17,733 --> 00:16:19,600
I got about 800 head
grazing down there.

381
00:16:19,600 --> 00:16:20,733
[Joel]
That's a whole lot of head.

382
00:16:20,733 --> 00:16:22,533
I bought my ranch with
the flight pay I saved up

383
00:16:22,533 --> 00:16:23,833
-during the war.
-Um-hmm.

384
00:16:23,833 --> 00:16:25,533
How does your wife feel
about you going?

385
00:16:25,533 --> 00:16:27,400
Well, she's a Texan, too.

386
00:16:27,400 --> 00:16:28,633
[Joel] That means
she's rock stupid.

387
00:16:28,633 --> 00:16:30,467
She knows that when a Texas man
makes up his mind

388
00:16:30,467 --> 00:16:32,400
to do something, that's it.

389
00:16:32,400 --> 00:16:33,967
Period. [laughs]

390
00:16:33,967 --> 00:16:35,800
I wish you fellows
could have seen her face

391
00:16:35,800 --> 00:16:38,933
so when I walked in and told her
in the strictest confidence,

392
00:16:38,933 --> 00:16:42,100
of course,
"Honey, I'm going to the moon."

393
00:16:42,100 --> 00:16:44,033
-What did she say?
-[Joel] Not tonight, dear.

394
00:16:44,033 --> 00:16:45,067
I've got a headache.

395
00:16:45,067 --> 00:16:46,467
[William] She looked at me
for a second

396
00:16:46,467 --> 00:16:47,633
and then said, "What for?

397
00:16:47,633 --> 00:16:49,400
They ain't got nothing there
that we don't have more of

398
00:16:49,400 --> 00:16:51,333
-right here in Texas."
-[laughing]

399
00:16:51,333 --> 00:16:52,533
[Crow] But of course,
by that time

400
00:16:52,533 --> 00:16:54,600
our lungs were aching for air.

401
00:16:54,833 --> 00:16:56,100
In the training room.

402
00:16:57,000 --> 00:16:58,600
I've done more flying
this last month

403
00:16:58,600 --> 00:17:00,067
than I've done
in over 100 missions.

404
00:17:00,067 --> 00:17:02,000
[Tom] Uh, great.
Now about your hair.

405
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,333
This is the hottest crew
I have ever worked with,

406
00:17:04,633 --> 00:17:06,400
especially
in the brains department.

407
00:17:06,667 --> 00:17:08,767
-[all] Nice brains.
-[Tom] Attractive, too.

408
00:17:08,767 --> 00:17:10,000
Hubba hubba.

409
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:11,267
Yeah, I agree.

410
00:17:11,267 --> 00:17:13,367
-But you can quote me on this.
-[Joel] Oh, may I?

411
00:17:13,367 --> 00:17:16,033
Unless you look like a test tube
or a chemical formula,

412
00:17:16,033 --> 00:17:17,600
-you haven't got a chance.
-[Tom] And I do.

413
00:17:17,600 --> 00:17:19,600
From the woman's angle,
Dr. Van Horn,

414
00:17:19,600 --> 00:17:22,833
how does it feel making a trip
like this alone with four men?

415
00:17:22,833 --> 00:17:25,533
[Tom] Do the words
"dream come true" ring a bell?

416
00:17:25,900 --> 00:17:27,500
I never thought much about it.

417
00:17:27,900 --> 00:17:30,367
Tell me, doctor, is there
any specific reason

418
00:17:30,367 --> 00:17:32,433
why one member of the crew
should be a woman?

419
00:17:32,733 --> 00:17:34,033
I'd like to answer that
if I may.

420
00:17:34,033 --> 00:17:35,167
[Tom] Hmm.

421
00:17:35,167 --> 00:17:37,200
The reason Ms. Van Horn
is making this trip is because

422
00:17:37,200 --> 00:17:39,767
of her pioneering research
with monotonic hydrogen.

423
00:17:39,767 --> 00:17:41,167
[Crow]
And she's cute as a button.

424
00:17:41,167 --> 00:17:42,500
...develop the
first rocket fuel,

425
00:17:42,500 --> 00:17:45,700
powerful and concentrated enough
to make this flight possible.

426
00:17:45,700 --> 00:17:47,700
[PA] X minus 11 minutes.

427
00:17:47,700 --> 00:17:50,100
[Joel] Uh, astronauts
get to the rocket.

428
00:17:50,433 --> 00:17:52,500
You may view the takeoff
from the observation bunker

429
00:17:52,500 --> 00:17:54,800
and return to this room
immediately afterward.

430
00:17:55,167 --> 00:17:58,100
Everybody return here
after the takeoff.

431
00:17:58,100 --> 00:17:59,500
[Crow]
Uh, accept the astronauts.

432
00:17:59,500 --> 00:18:00,767
We need them on the rocket.

433
00:18:00,767 --> 00:18:03,200
[Tom] Are you an astronaut
if you're wearing dungarees,

434
00:18:03,200 --> 00:18:04,933
and you've been training
for the last two years?

435
00:18:04,933 --> 00:18:06,900
[Joel] No. I can't remember.
Were we supposed to stay?

436
00:18:06,900 --> 00:18:08,667
[Tom] No. I think we're
supposed to go to the rocket.

437
00:18:08,667 --> 00:18:10,133
-[Tom] He said...
-[Crow] Was it this way?

438
00:18:10,133 --> 00:18:11,667
[Joel] He said astronauts
straight back.

439
00:18:11,667 --> 00:18:13,567
-[Crow] I'm an astronaut.
-[Tom] Wanna stop for ribs?

440
00:18:13,567 --> 00:18:15,133
[Joel] Hello, Cleveland.

441
00:18:15,800 --> 00:18:17,800
[PA]
X minus seven minutes.

442
00:18:17,800 --> 00:18:19,633
[Tom] Oh, great.
That's the support team?

443
00:18:19,633 --> 00:18:21,000
Three guys and a woody.

444
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:22,133
[Crow] Hey, I loved that movie.

445
00:18:22,133 --> 00:18:24,067
[man] Gyro control
and compass are okay.

446
00:18:24,433 --> 00:18:26,600
Batteries are all up,
autopilot's okay.

447
00:18:26,600 --> 00:18:27,800
Everything's in order.

448
00:18:27,800 --> 00:18:29,633
[Joel] Yeah, thanks for sharing.

449
00:18:29,633 --> 00:18:32,067
I've checked these calculations
many times. They're correct.

450
00:18:32,067 --> 00:18:33,267
[Tom] I'll be the judge of that.

451
00:18:33,267 --> 00:18:34,633
[man 1] Oh yes,
all commercial flights

452
00:18:34,633 --> 00:18:35,800
have been detoured
100 miles south.

453
00:18:35,800 --> 00:18:36,833
[man 2] Thank you.

454
00:18:38,133 --> 00:18:40,300
Well, I can't think of anything
we've overlooked.

455
00:18:40,300 --> 00:18:42,200
All we need now
is a little luck.

456
00:18:42,200 --> 00:18:44,433
Doesn't seem real that
the moment finally has arrived

457
00:18:44,433 --> 00:18:45,700
after all these years.

458
00:18:45,700 --> 00:18:47,233
It has been a long time.

459
00:18:47,233 --> 00:18:49,100
A long time for
two men to work together.

460
00:18:49,100 --> 00:18:50,167
-[whistling]
-[coughing]

461
00:18:50,167 --> 00:18:52,267
Remember when we started,
what they called us?

462
00:18:52,267 --> 00:18:53,733
-[Joel] Siegfried and Roy?
-Young crackpots.

463
00:18:53,733 --> 00:18:55,200
Yes, and what are now?

464
00:18:55,200 --> 00:18:57,467
-[Joel] Siegfried and Roy?
-Maybe just crackpots.

465
00:18:58,067 --> 00:19:00,767
The only regret I have
is that I have to stay behind.

466
00:19:01,167 --> 00:19:03,533
Your job is no less important,
Robert.

467
00:19:03,533 --> 00:19:04,833
I know, but still.

468
00:19:04,833 --> 00:19:07,000
-[PA] X minus six minutes.
-[Crow] We'll have Paris.

469
00:19:08,300 --> 00:19:10,700
Look, you're getting on that
rocket and that's all there is.

470
00:19:10,700 --> 00:19:11,767
Good luck.

471
00:19:13,167 --> 00:19:14,267
[man 1] Good luck, everybody.

472
00:19:14,867 --> 00:19:16,933
[Joel] Uh, can I catch
a ride to the rocket?

473
00:19:16,933 --> 00:19:18,533
I'm only going to the gantry.

474
00:19:18,533 --> 00:19:19,633
[Tom] Gantry?

475
00:19:19,633 --> 00:19:22,133
This car don't go anywhere
near a gantry.

476
00:19:22,133 --> 00:19:24,167
You boys is lost. [laughing]

477
00:19:24,167 --> 00:19:25,767
Fat old dog, ain't you?

478
00:19:26,767 --> 00:19:28,467
[Crow German accent]
Here in Peenemünde is where

479
00:19:28,467 --> 00:19:31,667
rockets are assembled
and once the rockets go up,

480
00:19:31,667 --> 00:19:33,267
who cares where they come down?

481
00:19:33,267 --> 00:19:35,200
That's not my department,
you know.

482
00:19:35,200 --> 00:19:39,700
[Tom, Crow, Joel] ♪ Valderi,
Valdera, Valderi, Valdera ♪

483
00:19:42,433 --> 00:19:45,167
[Joel] Can I still bet
on Max Schmeling?

484
00:19:45,633 --> 00:19:48,133
-[PA] X minus five minutes.
-Oh, good.

485
00:19:48,133 --> 00:19:52,633
[Tom, Crow, Joel] ♪ Valderi,
Valdera, Valderi, Valdera ♪

486
00:19:55,533 --> 00:19:57,133
[Tom] Well, five minutes to go.

487
00:19:57,133 --> 00:19:59,300
There's no rush,
as long as they're close.

488
00:19:59,300 --> 00:20:00,867
I mean,
this ain't rocket science.

489
00:20:00,867 --> 00:20:02,200
[Dr. Eckstrom]
Take one more look.

490
00:20:02,200 --> 00:20:03,467
[Tom spitting]

491
00:20:03,467 --> 00:20:06,300
You won't be as close as this
to mother Earth for some time.

492
00:20:07,933 --> 00:20:10,167
-[Crow] Just get on the rocket!
-[Joel] Come on.

493
00:20:11,100 --> 00:20:14,200
Hey, look. It looks like an
Al Jaffee
Mad Magazine fold-in,

494
00:20:14,200 --> 00:20:16,167
you know you can
fold it together.

495
00:20:16,467 --> 00:20:18,567
It would probably
spell something like Ohio or...

496
00:20:18,567 --> 00:20:19,933
We keep the motors inn high...

497
00:20:19,933 --> 00:20:22,367
-[Crow] Huh?
-[Joel] Hm? What'd he say?

498
00:20:23,700 --> 00:20:26,033
[Tom] All right, come on.
One at a time. Don't crowd.

499
00:20:26,033 --> 00:20:27,167
Don't crowd.

500
00:20:28,300 --> 00:20:29,533
Go on up there, Joel.

501
00:20:30,400 --> 00:20:33,300
[Crow] Hey, that-- that's
what it's like to be born.

502
00:20:33,300 --> 00:20:35,100
[Joel] They're coming up
through the toilet.

503
00:20:36,300 --> 00:20:38,233
I'm positive, yeah.

504
00:20:41,367 --> 00:20:45,067
[Tom] Close the lid.
There's a lady on board, please.

505
00:20:51,233 --> 00:20:52,267
[Crow] Well...

506
00:20:52,933 --> 00:20:54,767
[PA] X minus four minutes.

507
00:20:57,700 --> 00:20:59,767
[Crow] Last one out of the biff
is a rotten egg.

508
00:21:00,867 --> 00:21:02,367
Oh, that's you Bill.
[laughs]

509
00:21:02,367 --> 00:21:04,500
I was about to cut you up
with a coat hanger.

510
00:21:05,433 --> 00:21:06,900
[Tom] Call me Cobra.

511
00:21:07,933 --> 00:21:09,967
[Joel] Put a new float
while I was down there.

512
00:21:09,967 --> 00:21:12,667
[scoffs] Cut me up
with a coat hanger.

513
00:21:13,400 --> 00:21:15,267
[Crow] Hey, that guy's
not kidding. Nag, nag, nag.

514
00:21:15,267 --> 00:21:17,133
No change
of flight plans discussed.

515
00:21:17,533 --> 00:21:18,900
First seven minutes of flight...

516
00:21:18,900 --> 00:21:21,333
-Controlled by automatic pilot.
-Pilot's ready.

517
00:21:21,333 --> 00:21:22,633
[Joel] But he has
a question for you.

518
00:21:22,633 --> 00:21:24,400
Seven minutes...
straight ascent.

519
00:21:24,600 --> 00:21:26,267
Straight ascent
from starting point.

520
00:21:26,967 --> 00:21:29,500
Starting thrust using
all tail assembly engines,

521
00:21:29,500 --> 00:21:31,233
-twenty-three hundred tons.
-Right.

522
00:21:32,267 --> 00:21:34,000
[Tom] Straight, no chaser,
twist of lime.

523
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:35,100
Your mixture?

524
00:21:35,967 --> 00:21:38,167
Hydrogen and oxygen plus A12.

525
00:21:38,367 --> 00:21:42,500
After 120 seconds,
hydrogen and oxygen plus A14.

526
00:21:42,500 --> 00:21:45,633
After 340 seconds,
hydrogen plus A16.

527
00:21:45,633 --> 00:21:48,700
-After 560 seconds, A16.
-Right.

528
00:21:48,700 --> 00:21:50,633
-Any questions?
-[Crow] Yeah, what's A16?

529
00:21:50,633 --> 00:21:52,267
[PA] X minus two minutes.

530
00:21:52,933 --> 00:21:54,533
[Tom] I resent him.

531
00:21:55,067 --> 00:21:56,100
Good luck.

532
00:21:58,867 --> 00:22:01,433
[Joel] I'm bushed.
I'm turning in.

533
00:22:02,867 --> 00:22:05,000
[Tom] I'll do the honors,
little lady.

534
00:22:08,767 --> 00:22:10,700
[Crow] Good night,
Rockford Dad.

535
00:22:15,767 --> 00:22:18,800
[Joel] Good night, Mike Nelson
of Sea Hunt fame.

536
00:22:20,033 --> 00:22:22,033
[Tom] Good night,
guy who looks like Mr. Whipple,

537
00:22:22,033 --> 00:22:24,600
a little like Walt Disney,
maybe Mr. Mooney.

538
00:22:28,733 --> 00:22:30,800
-[PA] X minus one minute.
-[Joel] Good night, oven clock.

539
00:22:31,167 --> 00:22:33,033
[Tom] Good night,
guys in the bunker.

540
00:22:34,033 --> 00:22:35,567
[Crow] Good night,
junior high school

541
00:22:35,567 --> 00:22:37,800
custodian closet like room.

542
00:22:40,467 --> 00:22:42,733
[Joel]
Did I leave the water running?

543
00:22:42,733 --> 00:22:44,833
[Tom] Oh God, I need this job.

544
00:22:45,100 --> 00:22:47,200
[PA] X minus 40 seconds.

545
00:22:47,400 --> 00:22:49,700
[Joel] Sure hope Jimmy
brought the Camaro in.

546
00:22:50,200 --> 00:22:51,967
[PA] X minus 30 seconds.

547
00:22:51,967 --> 00:22:54,800
[Crow] By this time,
my lungs were aching for air.

548
00:22:54,800 --> 00:22:56,667
[PA] X minus 20 seconds.

549
00:22:56,667 --> 00:22:58,733
[Tom] I hope this new chin
holds out.

550
00:22:59,700 --> 00:23:01,700
[PA] X minus ten seconds.

551
00:23:03,067 --> 00:23:06,333
[Tom] They're at the post,
and they're off.

552
00:23:06,333 --> 00:23:08,500
[PA] Three, two, one...

553
00:23:08,500 --> 00:23:09,867
[Joel] Muffins are done.

554
00:23:10,133 --> 00:23:11,133
[PA] Zero.

555
00:23:17,500 --> 00:23:19,167
[Tom] Stock footage away.

556
00:23:21,400 --> 00:23:22,400
[Crow] Hmm.

557
00:23:22,767 --> 00:23:25,367
[Joel] It's great.

558
00:23:25,367 --> 00:23:27,267
[Crow snoring]

559
00:23:27,267 --> 00:23:29,200
[Tom] Can't zip my fly, ooh.

560
00:23:30,433 --> 00:23:32,433
[Crow] By this time, my lungs...

561
00:23:32,433 --> 00:23:34,300
[Joel] Buddy Ebsen
likes this ride.

562
00:23:34,967 --> 00:23:37,633
[Tom] Bye. Bye.

563
00:23:38,733 --> 00:23:41,200
[German accent]
Get out of my way. I can't see!

564
00:23:41,767 --> 00:23:43,900
[suspenseful music playing]

565
00:23:43,900 --> 00:23:45,767
[Tom] Bye.

566
00:23:46,200 --> 00:23:48,733
[Tom] ♪ Valderi, Valdera,
Valderi, Valdera ♪

567
00:23:50,933 --> 00:23:52,833
[Crow Well, that's a wrap.
Enjoy the buffet.

568
00:23:52,833 --> 00:23:55,900
We'll attempt to establish
radio contact with the ship.

569
00:23:56,267 --> 00:23:57,933
[Joel] The rest of the movie
takes place in space.

570
00:23:57,933 --> 00:23:59,233
You can all go home now.

571
00:23:59,233 --> 00:24:01,600
[Tom] Now they think of
radio contact.

572
00:24:02,733 --> 00:24:04,400
Those are little miles.

573
00:24:08,033 --> 00:24:10,067
And those are big miles.

574
00:24:14,267 --> 00:24:15,700
-[Crow] Good morning.
-[yawning]

575
00:24:15,700 --> 00:24:17,333
-[Joel] Morning, everybody.
-[Tom] Morning.

576
00:24:17,333 --> 00:24:18,667
[Tom] Rise and shine.

577
00:24:19,600 --> 00:24:22,067
The human body can
withstand these accelerations,

578
00:24:22,667 --> 00:24:24,300
but it certainly
was never meant to.

579
00:24:24,300 --> 00:24:26,133
[Crow] What did you expect?
You're an astronaut, Dicky.

580
00:24:26,133 --> 00:24:28,267
Feel like I was just tossed
off the spooky bronc.

581
00:24:28,267 --> 00:24:29,967
[Joel] I just ate my own vomit.

582
00:24:30,333 --> 00:24:31,567
[Van Horn] Are you all right,
doctor?

583
00:24:31,567 --> 00:24:33,633
Oh, yes. Can't hurt
an old campaigner like me.

584
00:24:33,633 --> 00:24:36,100
[Tom] Unless you bang my head
into a steel grate.

585
00:24:36,933 --> 00:24:38,867
Radar and gyro compass in order.

586
00:24:39,800 --> 00:24:41,333
[Floyd] Automatic pilot, okay.

587
00:24:41,667 --> 00:24:43,667
-Fuel consumption?
-Normal.

588
00:24:43,667 --> 00:24:45,500
-[Crow] Spinach quiche?
-[Tom] Almost done.

589
00:24:45,500 --> 00:24:46,900
Fifteen pounds.

590
00:24:50,833 --> 00:24:51,967
[Tom] Let's go.

591
00:24:51,967 --> 00:24:53,600
-We gotta get out of here.
-[Crow] Oh, yeah.

592
00:24:53,600 --> 00:24:56,333
[Tom] Suppose they had a war
and nobody came.

593
00:24:56,700 --> 00:24:58,400
Where do you want to be
in two years?

594
00:25:15,633 --> 00:25:17,733
-We at the Satellite of Love...
-[Crow, Tom] Doom doom doom...

595
00:25:17,733 --> 00:25:18,867
...take this time

596
00:25:18,867 --> 00:25:21,700
to salute some of the
unsung heroes of today's film.

597
00:25:22,100 --> 00:25:25,367
[Crow, Tom] The reporters
of Rocketship X-M.

598
00:25:25,367 --> 00:25:27,400
-They are the unsung heroes...
-[Crow, Tom] Doom doom doom...

599
00:25:27,400 --> 00:25:29,600
...that cuddle in back rooms,

600
00:25:29,600 --> 00:25:32,067
nameless like so many cattle.

601
00:25:32,300 --> 00:25:34,100
Uh, they are...

602
00:25:34,433 --> 00:25:36,200
slightly out of focus

603
00:25:36,200 --> 00:25:39,933
and we hold forth that
they should not be forgotten.

604
00:25:39,933 --> 00:25:41,067
[Crow, Joel] Doom doom...

605
00:25:41,067 --> 00:25:42,467
[Tom] They are the journalists,

606
00:25:42,467 --> 00:25:44,600
the plucky personnel
of the press corps

607
00:25:44,600 --> 00:25:46,233
whose mighty pens and pencils

608
00:25:46,233 --> 00:25:48,967
dutifully record
whatever drivel they are fed.

609
00:25:48,967 --> 00:25:52,033
Proud and passionate, and
blessed by the elegant beauty

610
00:25:52,033 --> 00:25:54,367
of the United States
space program.

611
00:25:54,633 --> 00:25:57,100
-[Crow] And so we present to you
-[Joel hums]

612
00:25:57,100 --> 00:25:59,900
the journalists of
Rocketship X-M.

613
00:25:59,900 --> 00:26:02,100
Um, roll them slides, Cam-bot.

614
00:26:03,533 --> 00:26:05,900
[Joel] Meet correspondent
Donald Bunsen,

615
00:26:05,900 --> 00:26:08,433
science editor
for the Allen Meyer Flash.

616
00:26:08,633 --> 00:26:11,133
His scathing seven-part series
on stop-cocks

617
00:26:11,133 --> 00:26:16,767
almost won him the
1953 Pulitzer Prize 80626.

618
00:26:16,767 --> 00:26:20,267
[Tom] The famous twin reporting
team of Dirk and Kirk Berger who

619
00:26:20,267 --> 00:26:22,400
-cover both sides of a story,
-[Joel humming]

620
00:26:22,400 --> 00:26:25,100
Dirk upfront
and Kirk always from behind.

621
00:26:25,100 --> 00:26:27,367
Their style is like
an Oreo cookie.

622
00:26:27,367 --> 00:26:29,367
Hard biting reporters
on the outside,

623
00:26:29,367 --> 00:26:31,667
a creamy story in between.

624
00:26:32,433 --> 00:26:35,167
[Crow] On the left,
Hugh Beaumont's brother Spike,

625
00:26:35,167 --> 00:26:38,167
hairstyle editor for
Today's Rugged Man.

626
00:26:38,167 --> 00:26:40,767
The boys in the city room say
Spike can sniff out

627
00:26:40,767 --> 00:26:44,100
an ounce of pomade
from inside a locked closet.

628
00:26:44,400 --> 00:26:47,733
Notice Spike has donned
his flying winged lapel suit

629
00:26:47,733 --> 00:26:50,333
in preparation
for today's plumb assignment,

630
00:26:50,333 --> 00:26:53,967
the trendsetting hair helmet
of Lloyd Bridges.

631
00:26:54,833 --> 00:26:56,233
[Joel] Peering over
Spike's left shoulder

632
00:26:56,233 --> 00:26:58,900
is foreign affairs correspondent
for Digest Digest

633
00:26:58,900 --> 00:27:01,633
Peter K. Whimple, mild-mannered
and meek to a fault

634
00:27:01,633 --> 00:27:07,167
Whipple's motto is "When will it
be my turn" now 91807.

635
00:27:07,733 --> 00:27:10,633
[Tom] Ladies Home Foundation
Garments is well represented

636
00:27:10,633 --> 00:27:13,000
-by cub reporter Robert Bland.
-[Joel, Crow humming]

637
00:27:13,000 --> 00:27:14,933
Here we see him
gathering information

638
00:27:14,933 --> 00:27:18,067
for his upcoming piece
on zero gravity girdles.

639
00:27:18,067 --> 00:27:20,767
Don't let that flint
hard exterior fool you.

640
00:27:20,767 --> 00:27:22,600
He's wearing a Warner bra,

641
00:27:22,600 --> 00:27:24,967
trimmed with delicate
peak-a-boo lace.

642
00:27:25,633 --> 00:27:28,300
[Crow] Leering in from the back
is chief editor of

643
00:27:28,300 --> 00:27:31,167
White Male Perspective,
Wilhelm Studman.

644
00:27:31,167 --> 00:27:32,967
Visibly upset with the intrusion

645
00:27:32,967 --> 00:27:35,400
of a mere girl
into a man's world.

646
00:27:35,400 --> 00:27:36,933
Studman keeps his distance

647
00:27:36,933 --> 00:27:40,167
because Chanel No. 5
gives him hives. [chuckles]

648
00:27:40,467 --> 00:27:43,900
The boys in the press corps have
nicknamed him The Gollum.

649
00:27:43,900 --> 00:27:48,000
[Joel] And there's so many more.
There's Billy, Frank, and Judy.

650
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:50,900
[Tom] Fast Talking Fanny
and Automatic Slim.

651
00:27:51,233 --> 00:27:53,767
[Crow] There's Roy and Scoop
and Cold Type.

652
00:27:53,967 --> 00:27:56,033
[Joel] All Man,
Mr. Peepers and The Leech.

653
00:27:56,033 --> 00:27:57,767
[Tom] And this guy.
He's a Woozle.

654
00:27:57,767 --> 00:27:59,433
His name is peanut.

655
00:28:00,200 --> 00:28:02,733
These are the men and women
of the press corps

656
00:28:02,733 --> 00:28:05,267
who will do anything
for a story.

657
00:28:05,267 --> 00:28:07,367
Unless they're told
to withhold information.

658
00:28:07,900 --> 00:28:12,133
[all] We salute the press corps
of Rocketship X-M.

659
00:28:12,133 --> 00:28:14,133
Excelsior!

660
00:28:14,567 --> 00:28:17,200
[music playing]

661
00:28:28,633 --> 00:28:30,000
[Joel] What's your dream?

662
00:28:31,367 --> 00:28:32,367
Harry.

663
00:28:44,767 --> 00:28:46,867
-Marvelous site, isn't it?
-It is.

664
00:28:48,167 --> 00:28:51,067
You study maps,
globes and try to visualize.

665
00:28:51,533 --> 00:28:52,800
[Joel] Then you get up in space

666
00:28:53,100 --> 00:28:54,700
and... God,
your hair smells good.

667
00:28:54,700 --> 00:28:56,000
[Harry] Hard to express it.

668
00:28:58,533 --> 00:28:59,900
[Dr. Eckstrom] Stand by to turn.

669
00:29:00,200 --> 00:29:02,533
[Crow] Like this?
Or like that?

670
00:29:02,533 --> 00:29:03,700
Like that? Okay. I got it.

671
00:29:03,700 --> 00:29:05,933
[Tom] Hoist the mizzen,
Mr. Cavendish!

672
00:29:05,933 --> 00:29:07,700
Knot the main sheet.
Blow me down.

673
00:29:08,267 --> 00:29:11,100
-Four, three...
-[Joel] Five.

674
00:29:11,100 --> 00:29:13,067
-[Dr. Eckstrom] Two. Turn
-[Joel] Twelve.

675
00:29:17,400 --> 00:29:20,800
[Tom] Look smart, me ladies.
Harrr mee bonny tarrrrs.

676
00:29:20,800 --> 00:29:21,967
[Crow] Weigh that anchor.

677
00:29:22,300 --> 00:29:23,833
-[Tom] Yeah.
-[Crow] I can’t even lift it.

678
00:29:23,833 --> 00:29:27,700
[Tom singing]
♪ Where is love? ♪

679
00:29:27,700 --> 00:29:29,967
♪ Is it in the stars? ♪

680
00:29:33,433 --> 00:29:34,700
[William] Ninety-degree turn
completed.

681
00:29:35,667 --> 00:29:39,100
She will have a flight speed
of 3,400 miles per hour.

682
00:29:39,367 --> 00:29:41,400
Altitude 360 miles.

683
00:29:41,633 --> 00:29:43,067
[Van Horn] How do we stand
on fuel now?

684
00:29:43,067 --> 00:29:44,133
[Crow] I'm for it.

685
00:29:45,400 --> 00:29:47,167
-[Van Horn] Mixture at present.
-[William] A16.

686
00:29:47,167 --> 00:29:48,567
Harry, contact the base.

687
00:29:48,567 --> 00:29:51,700
[Crow] Oh, Gary, ahh.

688
00:29:58,567 --> 00:30:01,100
RXM calling BWS.

689
00:30:01,300 --> 00:30:03,300
RXM calling BWS.

690
00:30:03,300 --> 00:30:04,367
Over.

691
00:30:04,367 --> 00:30:06,500
[mane on radio] Come in, RXM.
Over to you.

692
00:30:06,500 --> 00:30:08,833
-[Joel] No. Over to you.
-Standby BWS.

693
00:30:11,333 --> 00:30:12,433
Eckstrom speaking.

694
00:30:13,100 --> 00:30:15,300
[Joel] The press is
a bunch of wusses.

695
00:30:15,300 --> 00:30:16,933
Are you alone in there?

696
00:30:17,333 --> 00:30:21,133
[radio] And are circling the
globe at 3,400 miles per hour.

697
00:30:21,867 --> 00:30:25,167
We will increase speed gradually
until we reach escape velocity

698
00:30:25,167 --> 00:30:27,300
of 25,000 miles per hour.

699
00:30:27,300 --> 00:30:30,500
[Joel] Oh, traffic on the I94
is heavy today...

700
00:30:30,500 --> 00:30:32,700
and it seems to be jamming up
along Spaghetti Junction.

701
00:30:34,433 --> 00:30:35,900
We'll be standing by.

702
00:30:35,900 --> 00:30:38,133
-[Tom] Tall and proud.
-Fifty-five hundred miles.

703
00:30:38,400 --> 00:30:39,767
Continue acceleration.

704
00:30:40,567 --> 00:30:43,300
[Crow] Oh, bless the beast
and children.

705
00:30:45,433 --> 00:30:47,333
[Tom] One tin soldier.

706
00:30:47,333 --> 00:30:50,100
[Joel] Go placidly
amidst the noise and haste

707
00:30:50,100 --> 00:30:52,100
and remember what comfort?

708
00:30:53,033 --> 00:30:54,433
It's the Chrysler building.

709
00:31:02,900 --> 00:31:04,533
Hey, what happened
to the rest of the night?

710
00:31:04,533 --> 00:31:07,100
[Joel] Hey, who left his post?

711
00:31:07,733 --> 00:31:10,333
If it's darkness you want,
wait until we enter outer space.

712
00:31:10,333 --> 00:31:12,200
[Crow]
And then look into my soul.

713
00:31:12,200 --> 00:31:13,567
Six thousand.

714
00:31:13,567 --> 00:31:15,800
Six thousand two-hundred
miles per hour.

715
00:31:17,967 --> 00:31:21,367
[Joel] Come on, look alive!
You're getting scale. Let's go!

716
00:31:21,867 --> 00:31:23,667
Radar is tracking
them perfectly.

717
00:31:23,667 --> 00:31:25,667
I had contact with them
four minutes ago.

718
00:31:26,367 --> 00:31:30,067
Altitude 1600 miles,
speed 21,000 miles per hour,

719
00:31:30,067 --> 00:31:31,533
constantly increasing.

720
00:31:31,533 --> 00:31:32,933
They're about to reach
escape velocity.

721
00:31:32,933 --> 00:31:34,933
-[Tom] I win the baby pool.
-Twenty-two thousand.

722
00:31:36,500 --> 00:31:39,267
Bill, check the pressurizing
system and oxygen.

723
00:31:40,233 --> 00:31:42,133
This kind of weather
makes me feel right at home.

724
00:31:42,133 --> 00:31:43,300
[Crow]
You mean this musty stench.

725
00:31:43,300 --> 00:31:45,333
Like a nice cool night
in East Texas.

726
00:31:45,333 --> 00:31:46,467
Twenty-four thousand.

727
00:31:46,467 --> 00:31:48,300
Take a reading the moment
we attain escape velocity.

728
00:31:48,300 --> 00:31:49,633
-Yes, sir.
-Standby.

729
00:31:49,633 --> 00:31:51,033
[Tom] Why?
Is something gonna happen?

730
00:31:51,033 --> 00:31:53,000
Should we get ready
for the boring part now?

731
00:31:53,000 --> 00:31:54,000
Twenty-five thousand.

732
00:31:54,367 --> 00:31:56,100
Prepare to jettison
tail section.

733
00:31:56,100 --> 00:31:57,167
[Tom] Oh, wow.

734
00:31:57,167 --> 00:31:58,633
Start the front assembly motors.

735
00:31:58,633 --> 00:32:00,433
Only 40 second
supply of fuel left.

736
00:32:00,967 --> 00:32:02,067
[whistling sound]

737
00:32:02,067 --> 00:32:05,733
[Crow] Oh. Was that me?
Dehydrated ice cream. Sorry.

738
00:32:05,733 --> 00:32:06,900
You ready?

739
00:32:06,900 --> 00:32:07,933
Ready.

740
00:32:08,300 --> 00:32:09,300
Go.

741
00:32:12,800 --> 00:32:14,100
Reduce power.

742
00:32:15,933 --> 00:32:17,767
[Tom] Dad, stop!
We lost the camper!

743
00:32:17,767 --> 00:32:18,767
Look out!

744
00:32:26,433 --> 00:32:28,467
[Joel] Well, we won't
be trying that again.

745
00:32:28,467 --> 00:32:30,433
That was a little
too close for comfort.

746
00:32:30,767 --> 00:32:33,867
We might be in vacuum, but I
sure felt the wind of that one.

747
00:32:33,867 --> 00:32:35,000
[Tom] Uh, no. That was me.

748
00:32:35,000 --> 00:32:36,733
Harry, contact the base again.

749
00:32:41,900 --> 00:32:44,233
[Crow] Attention, base.
Snafu big time.

750
00:32:44,233 --> 00:32:45,267
Over.

751
00:32:45,633 --> 00:32:47,833
[man on radio] Come in, RXM.
Over to you.

752
00:32:47,833 --> 00:32:49,533
Standby BWS.

753
00:32:49,533 --> 00:32:50,733
[Tom] Give me that.

754
00:32:51,567 --> 00:32:53,233
We're on our way.

755
00:32:53,800 --> 00:32:56,433
[man on radio] We have
jettisoned the tail section

756
00:32:56,433 --> 00:32:58,767
and are now reducing
power and speed

757
00:32:58,767 --> 00:33:00,500
according to plan

758
00:33:00,500 --> 00:33:03,767
until we come within
the attraction of the moon.

759
00:33:03,767 --> 00:33:06,800
[Joel] That's my man
up there, and yes,

760
00:33:06,800 --> 00:33:08,100
I think I love him.

761
00:33:10,033 --> 00:33:12,200
[man on radio]
Everybody aboard is well.

762
00:33:12,200 --> 00:33:13,700
[Crow] This is Rocketship X-M

763
00:33:13,700 --> 00:33:16,100
now concluding
our broadcast day.

764
00:33:16,300 --> 00:33:18,600
BWS calling RXM.

765
00:33:19,033 --> 00:33:20,433
Come in, RXM!

766
00:33:20,433 --> 00:33:24,567
[Joel, Tom, Crow
humming "Star-Spangled Banner]

767
00:33:27,000 --> 00:33:28,967
[Joel] Come on, spread out.
Give me some air.

768
00:33:28,967 --> 00:33:30,167
What's the deal?

769
00:33:30,167 --> 00:33:32,633
[Fleming] Ladies and gentlemen,
we had hoped by having

770
00:33:32,633 --> 00:33:34,733
the entire press
represented here tonight,

771
00:33:35,000 --> 00:33:37,067
to eliminate any possible
hearsay, rumor

772
00:33:37,067 --> 00:33:38,867
or speculation
put in the reports.

773
00:33:39,300 --> 00:33:41,633
However, I regret to have
to have to ask you to confine

774
00:33:41,633 --> 00:33:43,500
yourselves to the
official news release.

775
00:33:43,500 --> 00:33:44,800
[Joel] And stop ranting about...

776
00:33:44,800 --> 00:33:46,933
I promise to hand you any news
that may come in

777
00:33:46,933 --> 00:33:48,133
as soon as it can be cleared.

778
00:33:48,133 --> 00:33:50,900
[Crow] In other words, your trip
here was a waste of time.

779
00:33:50,900 --> 00:33:52,233
You could have phoned it in.

780
00:33:53,433 --> 00:33:56,733
[Joel] Where do you want to be
in two years, snowflake?

781
00:33:58,433 --> 00:34:00,200
Don't think
I like all this dark.

782
00:34:01,467 --> 00:34:03,767
It might be all right
for sleeping,

783
00:34:04,267 --> 00:34:06,100
but for a steady diet, uh-uh.

784
00:34:06,333 --> 00:34:08,567
I used to hate the daylight
because I couldn't work.

785
00:34:09,567 --> 00:34:11,167
Up here in this eternal night,

786
00:34:11,767 --> 00:34:13,067
it's a different feeling.

787
00:34:13,833 --> 00:34:15,733
A fella can get used
to anything I guess if he...

788
00:34:15,733 --> 00:34:17,433
[Crow]
Yeah, once they ate woodchuck.

789
00:34:17,433 --> 00:34:20,200
I remember when
I was first assigned to jets,

790
00:34:20,200 --> 00:34:21,833
I said to the colonel,
"Colonel,

791
00:34:21,833 --> 00:34:24,500
I join this man's Air Force
to fly an airplane,

792
00:34:24,800 --> 00:34:27,367
but nobody's going to hitch me
to no Roman candle."

793
00:34:27,367 --> 00:34:29,767
[Crow] And nobody's going to
strap me to a diving board naked

794
00:34:29,767 --> 00:34:32,267
and face down
in a pool of marshmallow cream.

795
00:34:34,400 --> 00:34:36,500
Boy oh boy.

796
00:34:38,200 --> 00:34:39,367
Ain't she pretty?

797
00:34:39,633 --> 00:34:41,000
[Tom] What do you see, fella?

798
00:34:44,033 --> 00:34:46,400
[Joel] It says "Rand McNally"
across the top.

799
00:34:46,400 --> 00:34:47,500
Do you see Texas?

800
00:34:47,800 --> 00:34:49,700
No.
It's in the other hemisphere.

801
00:34:49,700 --> 00:34:50,900
[Joel] Tell me your dream.

802
00:34:50,900 --> 00:34:53,067
...from this distance it would
only appear a mere speck.

803
00:34:53,600 --> 00:34:54,900
A mere speck?

804
00:34:55,633 --> 00:34:57,233
Texas a mere speck?

805
00:34:58,367 --> 00:35:00,733
Listen, my friend,
I'm broadminded.

806
00:35:00,733 --> 00:35:02,700
I've been around people, but...

807
00:35:03,467 --> 00:35:06,500
don't you ever let any
other Texan here you say that.

808
00:35:07,033 --> 00:35:08,367
A mere speck!

809
00:35:08,633 --> 00:35:10,500
[Crow] Hey, it was
just an observation.

810
00:35:11,067 --> 00:35:13,167
[Joel] Uh, what's my line?

811
00:35:13,167 --> 00:35:15,300
Oh, I better
check my script here.

812
00:35:15,800 --> 00:35:16,800
Yeah. That's it.

813
00:35:20,033 --> 00:35:23,533
[Tom] Now I swing into high
and make my move.

814
00:35:23,533 --> 00:35:25,167
It's the funniest sensation.

815
00:35:25,733 --> 00:35:27,867
I feel like I'm walking
on a cloud, no effort at all.

816
00:35:27,867 --> 00:35:28,933
[Tom] Not for you.

817
00:35:28,933 --> 00:35:30,533
We're getting deeper
into interplanetary space.

818
00:35:30,533 --> 00:35:32,767
Gravity will soon be
practically nonexistent.

819
00:35:33,233 --> 00:35:35,600
-[Joel] Hmm.
-Huh. We got a stowaway aboard.

820
00:35:36,533 --> 00:35:37,667
[laughing]

821
00:35:37,867 --> 00:35:40,000
[Crow] Ha ha ha.

822
00:35:40,000 --> 00:35:41,833
-Hey, whoa.
-[Crow] I love this old jacket.

823
00:35:41,833 --> 00:35:42,967
I need you.

824
00:35:44,033 --> 00:35:46,233
[Tom] Hey, we really are
having fun, aren't we?

825
00:35:47,067 --> 00:35:48,633
[Floyd] Don't you think
it's amusing?

826
00:35:50,000 --> 00:35:52,767
Nothing funny about that.
Simply the lack of gravity.

827
00:35:52,767 --> 00:35:55,067
-[Tom] She's right.
-Of course, Dr. Van Horn.

828
00:35:56,233 --> 00:35:57,533
[Joel, tom, Crow laughing]

829
00:35:58,200 --> 00:36:00,167
[Crow] Those straps
get me every time.

830
00:36:00,700 --> 00:36:02,867
[Joel, tom, Crow laughing]

831
00:36:06,167 --> 00:36:10,533
[theme music playing]

832
00:36:11,767 --> 00:36:13,300
Now don't get mad at me.

833
00:36:13,300 --> 00:36:15,100
But can't you ever relax?

834
00:36:15,100 --> 00:36:17,000
All these weeks, months,
I've been watching you.

835
00:36:17,000 --> 00:36:18,633
[Tom] Yeah, take a week off.

836
00:36:22,000 --> 00:36:23,300
Well, I've been wondering.

837
00:36:23,600 --> 00:36:24,867
How does a girl like you

838
00:36:24,867 --> 00:36:27,167
get mixed up in a thing
like this in the first place?

839
00:36:27,167 --> 00:36:29,000
[Joel] Have to stand
next to a guy like me.

840
00:36:29,000 --> 00:36:31,000
I suppose you think
that women should only cook

841
00:36:31,000 --> 00:36:33,767
and sew and bear children?

842
00:36:34,400 --> 00:36:36,767
-[Crow snorting]
-Isn't that enough?

843
00:36:38,800 --> 00:36:40,600
There's such a thing
as going overboard

844
00:36:40,600 --> 00:36:42,333
in the other direction too,
you know.

845
00:36:43,767 --> 00:36:45,767
-If you don't...
-[explosion]

846
00:36:47,167 --> 00:36:49,733
[Tom] Hmm. What is it?

847
00:36:49,733 --> 00:36:50,733
Hmm.

848
00:36:51,500 --> 00:36:53,967
[Joel] Chauvinist detector
just went off.

849
00:36:54,967 --> 00:36:57,733
Oh, false alarm. It's just
the dryer. It's off kilter.

850
00:37:00,467 --> 00:37:01,667
I'll check the engines.

851
00:37:01,667 --> 00:37:03,600
Phil, pressurize
the motor room first.

852
00:37:03,600 --> 00:37:05,633
[suspenseful music playing]

853
00:37:05,633 --> 00:37:07,367
[Joel] Who turned on the radio?

854
00:37:09,100 --> 00:37:12,433
Would you shut that music off,
please?

855
00:37:13,633 --> 00:37:15,367
Motor room pressure up.

856
00:37:15,700 --> 00:37:17,233
[Crow] Uh, Houston,
we got a problem.

857
00:37:17,233 --> 00:37:19,900
Lloyd's making moves
on the babe here.

858
00:37:24,867 --> 00:37:26,633
[Joel] Oh man, if it's not
one thing, it's another.

859
00:37:26,633 --> 00:37:28,233
Who forgot to flush?

860
00:37:32,700 --> 00:37:35,433
[underwater sounding]
Wow. This is neat up here.

861
00:37:35,433 --> 00:37:38,433
We can make a fork,
start her all over.

862
00:37:54,000 --> 00:37:56,000
[Joel underwater] Let's see.

863
00:37:56,000 --> 00:37:58,967
This thing goes into
another thing

864
00:37:58,967 --> 00:38:02,667
and then comes out
somewhere over here.

865
00:38:02,967 --> 00:38:07,700
[Tom mumbles underwater]

866
00:38:11,267 --> 00:38:12,333
We're still drifting.

867
00:38:12,333 --> 00:38:13,800
[Crow] Off to sleep.

868
00:38:20,067 --> 00:38:22,233
[Joel underwater]
Well, we stared at it.

869
00:38:22,433 --> 00:38:24,167
That ought to fix it.

870
00:38:24,367 --> 00:38:26,333
Let's get out of here.

871
00:38:26,333 --> 00:38:27,467
[Tom underwater] Okay.

872
00:38:29,400 --> 00:38:31,600
After 14 hours and 12 minutes,

873
00:38:32,333 --> 00:38:34,700
we covered 112,000 miles.

874
00:38:35,067 --> 00:38:36,800
We'll come within
the gravitational traction

875
00:38:36,800 --> 00:38:38,433
-of the moon.
-That's not important right now.

876
00:38:38,433 --> 00:38:41,200
Without power, we're helplessly
suspended in space.

877
00:38:41,733 --> 00:38:43,867
Even if we were already
within the gravitational reach,

878
00:38:43,867 --> 00:38:45,867
we'd still be unable
to make a landing.

879
00:38:45,867 --> 00:38:47,700
We need power to land.

880
00:38:47,700 --> 00:38:49,867
Checked and double checked
all connections.

881
00:38:49,867 --> 00:38:52,167
Primers, fuel injectors,

882
00:38:52,567 --> 00:38:54,333
can't find a thing wrong.

883
00:38:54,667 --> 00:38:57,600
-[Joel] Hmm.
-[Tom] Hmm. Um-hm.

884
00:38:57,600 --> 00:38:59,167
It must
be the fuel mixture.

885
00:38:59,167 --> 00:39:00,633
[Joel] Hmm. Possible.

886
00:39:02,367 --> 00:39:03,900
-[Joel] Can't tell.
-[Tom] Yeah.

887
00:39:03,900 --> 00:39:06,600
[Crow] Dear Diary,
well, we're all going to die,

888
00:39:06,600 --> 00:39:07,967
and it's the men's fault.

889
00:39:07,967 --> 00:39:11,267
Our fiery demise is imminent,
but at least I have my health.

890
00:39:11,267 --> 00:39:12,533
Knock on wood.

891
00:39:12,533 --> 00:39:15,300
Oh, I saw the cutest thing
on Arsenio last night.

892
00:39:15,567 --> 00:39:17,167
How much oxygen do we carry?

893
00:39:17,900 --> 00:39:20,233
Don't worry. We have
plenty left for the trip.

894
00:39:22,100 --> 00:39:23,700
For the trip as planned,
you mean.

895
00:39:25,000 --> 00:39:26,633
[Joel] Oh, that hurt.

896
00:39:32,367 --> 00:39:34,533
[Tom humming]

897
00:39:36,467 --> 00:39:38,167
[Tom] Hmm? What's this?

898
00:39:38,167 --> 00:39:40,133
[Crow] Oh, great, a harmonica.

899
00:39:40,133 --> 00:39:42,567
As if this guy wasn't
annoying enough?

900
00:39:42,567 --> 00:39:44,233
Out with the harmonica.

901
00:39:44,233 --> 00:39:45,533
[Tom chuckles]

902
00:39:45,533 --> 00:39:48,133
What's next? Stories about
his uncle in Milwaukee.

903
00:39:48,133 --> 00:39:49,233
[Tom] Oops.

904
00:39:51,967 --> 00:39:53,467
[harmonica playing]

905
00:39:55,000 --> 00:39:56,333
If you don't mind.

906
00:39:57,233 --> 00:40:00,233
[Joel] Geez, what a grouch.

907
00:40:03,833 --> 00:40:06,233
The differential is six over M
to the 30th power.

908
00:40:06,233 --> 00:40:10,267
The halfway check result is
262,000 to 341,000

909
00:40:10,267 --> 00:40:11,900
both using tangent E, correct?

910
00:40:11,900 --> 00:40:14,500
[Joel] Huh? Oh, sorry.
I was drawing Bucky here.

911
00:40:14,800 --> 00:40:17,333
It must be the same.
There's an error there.

912
00:40:18,233 --> 00:40:20,267
I've made no error,
Dr. Eckstrom.

913
00:40:21,700 --> 00:40:24,333
I have to say that you made an
error and discard your figures.

914
00:40:24,600 --> 00:40:25,700
I'm sorry.

915
00:40:26,000 --> 00:40:27,567
-[Tom] Sheesh.
-Don't be.

916
00:40:28,400 --> 00:40:30,367
Surely, you're not going
to let emotion enter into this.

917
00:40:30,367 --> 00:40:32,200
-[Tom] Oh, why would I?
-Certainly not.

918
00:40:32,700 --> 00:40:35,600
We'll continue computing
using my results as a basis.

919
00:40:35,600 --> 00:40:37,233
[all] Blow hard.

920
00:40:37,833 --> 00:40:39,467
Except that I feel very strongly

921
00:40:39,467 --> 00:40:41,467
I should say that
we should try both.

922
00:40:42,000 --> 00:40:43,000
We can't.

923
00:40:43,333 --> 00:40:46,433
To complete either calculation
would take six to eight hours.

924
00:40:46,433 --> 00:40:47,900
We can't afford the time.

925
00:40:47,900 --> 00:40:49,600
It's either one or the other,
Dr. Van Horn.

926
00:40:49,967 --> 00:40:51,600
But it doesn't have to be.

927
00:40:51,600 --> 00:40:54,033
You can't be arbitrary
about imposing your will

928
00:40:54,033 --> 00:40:56,067
when these people's lives
are at stake.

929
00:40:56,067 --> 00:40:57,600
Don't you realize that?

930
00:40:58,500 --> 00:41:01,433
You speak as calmly as if
you were saying pass the salt.

931
00:41:01,833 --> 00:41:04,067
Aren't you human?
Are you made of ice?

932
00:41:04,267 --> 00:41:06,500
[Crow] I'm human.
Now shut up and pass the salt.

933
00:41:06,700 --> 00:41:08,767
I'm sorry. I apologize.

934
00:41:09,467 --> 00:41:12,200
For what?
For momentarily being a woman?

935
00:41:12,433 --> 00:41:15,633
[Joel] Thank you
Mr. White male reality.

936
00:41:15,633 --> 00:41:17,467
Now, shall we go ahead?

937
00:41:17,967 --> 00:41:19,000
Yes, doctor.

938
00:41:20,067 --> 00:41:21,400
Engine L9.

939
00:41:21,400 --> 00:41:23,533
[Joel] If we make
the eyes a little bigger.

940
00:41:24,500 --> 00:41:25,867
[Crow] Meanwhile, back on Earth,

941
00:41:25,867 --> 00:41:29,267
silos are bursting
with nature's rich bounty.

942
00:41:29,600 --> 00:41:31,467
[Tom] Mount Palomar, California,

943
00:41:31,467 --> 00:41:34,300
the mighty 200-inch
reflecting Hale telescope

944
00:41:34,300 --> 00:41:36,500
is lofted skyward.

945
00:41:38,133 --> 00:41:40,733
Then, the eyepiece is blackened,

946
00:41:40,733 --> 00:41:43,800
and an unsuspecting scientist
puts his eye to it.

947
00:41:43,800 --> 00:41:45,300
-Comedy ensues.
-[phone ringing]

948
00:41:45,300 --> 00:41:46,400
Sign here, Mr. Wilson.

949
00:41:46,400 --> 00:41:47,433
Hello?

950
00:41:48,167 --> 00:41:49,233
Yes?

951
00:41:50,333 --> 00:41:52,000
It's Dr. Hurley at Mount
Palomar.

952
00:41:52,667 --> 00:41:54,167
Yes, Dr. Hurley? Yes?

953
00:41:54,167 --> 00:41:58,167
We located the RXM's position
at 7:16 this morning.

954
00:41:58,167 --> 00:41:59,833
Since then, there has
been very little change.

955
00:41:59,833 --> 00:42:01,100
[Tom] Voice of Winnie the Pooh.

956
00:42:01,100 --> 00:42:02,933
[Dr. Hurley on the phone]
Apparently, their velocity

957
00:42:02,933 --> 00:42:05,200
was far more
than we anticipated.

958
00:42:05,200 --> 00:42:06,667
Are you sure, doctor?

959
00:42:06,667 --> 00:42:08,200
[Crow] I think everyone
ought to come in

960
00:42:08,200 --> 00:42:09,500
and have a hot cup of cocoa

961
00:42:09,500 --> 00:42:11,400
and come inside
and be nice and snugly.

962
00:42:11,400 --> 00:42:14,033
[music playing]

963
00:42:14,033 --> 00:42:16,367
[Tom]
I have some rather bad news.

964
00:42:16,367 --> 00:42:18,267
There's more film.

965
00:42:20,400 --> 00:42:21,933
[Joel] Say, I never climbed
inside this thing.

966
00:42:21,933 --> 00:42:23,000
Kind of funny in here.

967
00:42:23,000 --> 00:42:24,267
[Crow] Look through the big end.

968
00:42:24,533 --> 00:42:25,733
[Tom] See, it's warm.

969
00:42:28,167 --> 00:42:29,967
So, hmm.

970
00:42:30,667 --> 00:42:33,167
[Joel] This is what we would
have seen from the Hubble.

971
00:42:36,367 --> 00:42:39,433
[Crow] So I sing you
to sleep after the lovin'.

972
00:42:42,233 --> 00:42:44,433
The wind that blows
between the worlds,

973
00:42:44,433 --> 00:42:45,800
cutting like a knife.

974
00:42:45,800 --> 00:42:47,167
-[Tom] Um-hmm.
-[Van Horn] What?

975
00:42:47,900 --> 00:42:50,600
Nothing. It's a line
from Kipling's "Tomlinson".

976
00:42:50,600 --> 00:42:53,933
The poem about the chap's...
soul in a fix.

977
00:42:54,233 --> 00:42:55,967
He couldn't get
into Heaven or Hell.

978
00:42:56,800 --> 00:42:58,667
Suspended in space.

979
00:42:59,700 --> 00:43:01,367
Would you do something for me,
please?

980
00:43:01,700 --> 00:43:03,633
[Joel, Tom] Shut up?

981
00:43:03,633 --> 00:43:06,967
I need those two graphs
under Dr. Eckstrom's arm.

982
00:43:07,867 --> 00:43:10,167
I'm tired and shaky,
I might awaken him.

983
00:43:10,800 --> 00:43:13,467
[Tom] Yeah, and then put his
arm in a pan of lukewarm water.

984
00:43:13,467 --> 00:43:14,900
[snickering]

985
00:43:16,600 --> 00:43:18,567
I knocked over a ten foot
Christmas tree once

986
00:43:18,567 --> 00:43:20,167
while decorating
at my cousin's house

987
00:43:20,167 --> 00:43:22,533
just from trying to do something
when I was too tired.

988
00:43:22,533 --> 00:43:25,400
[Crow] Oh, what does this story
have to do with anything, Lloyd?

989
00:43:25,400 --> 00:43:26,900
...came rushing in.

990
00:43:27,233 --> 00:43:29,000
First, they thought I was
Santa Claus lying there

991
00:43:29,000 --> 00:43:31,433
with a busted tinsel star
in my nose.

992
00:43:31,433 --> 00:43:32,833
I spoiled everything.

993
00:43:34,467 --> 00:43:36,633
[Tom] Now you tell me
that's not funny.

994
00:43:39,200 --> 00:43:41,800
[Joel] He's waking up.
Quick! Tell that story again.

995
00:43:44,233 --> 00:43:45,867
-Thanks.
-It's no trouble.

996
00:43:50,600 --> 00:43:52,300
Why don't you take
a minute off from that?

997
00:43:52,300 --> 00:43:53,400
[Crow] She's pretending to work

998
00:43:53,400 --> 00:43:55,133
so she doesn't
have to talk to you.

999
00:43:55,500 --> 00:43:57,433
You'll think clearer if you do.

1000
00:44:02,367 --> 00:44:04,333
When I was flying in the war,
we had a guy like...

1001
00:44:04,333 --> 00:44:05,533
-[Crow] No!
-[Tom] Brother.

1002
00:44:05,533 --> 00:44:08,233
-[Joel] Shut up!
-[Crow] Boo!

1003
00:44:08,233 --> 00:44:09,367
Too bad.

1004
00:44:10,300 --> 00:44:11,433
He blew his top.

1005
00:44:11,667 --> 00:44:13,267
We had to put him
in section eight.

1006
00:44:14,467 --> 00:44:16,800
Held it in too long.
Never let down a second.

1007
00:44:18,067 --> 00:44:19,500
[Crow] Never tasted human flesh.

1008
00:44:19,500 --> 00:44:20,633
You're right.

1009
00:44:21,533 --> 00:44:23,767
I'm not even thinking
straight anymore.

1010
00:44:23,767 --> 00:44:25,400
[Joel] I must be tired.

1011
00:44:26,100 --> 00:44:28,533
I'm almost interested
in what you're talking about.

1012
00:44:28,533 --> 00:44:30,333
-[Tom] Let's get outta here.
-[Joel] I'll grab you.

1013
00:44:30,333 --> 00:44:31,500
There we go.

1014
00:44:32,567 --> 00:44:34,033
[Joel] Yep. Okay.

1015
00:44:34,333 --> 00:44:35,800
[Tom] It's about donut shapes.

1016
00:44:37,400 --> 00:44:40,167
[music playing]

1017
00:44:42,733 --> 00:44:45,367
Now did I or did I not address

1018
00:44:45,367 --> 00:44:47,433
the topic of selective gravity,
Mr. Crow?

1019
00:44:47,433 --> 00:44:52,367
Uh, well we did global
and what's the other thing?

1020
00:44:52,367 --> 00:44:53,533
-I don't...
-[Tom] Shh.

1021
00:44:53,533 --> 00:44:55,367
Mr. Crow,
please try to pay attention.

1022
00:44:55,367 --> 00:44:57,000
This is for your benefit.

1023
00:44:57,000 --> 00:44:58,300
Yes, sir. Sorry.

1024
00:44:58,300 --> 00:45:02,600
All right, selective gravity is
the ease of which fish line...

1025
00:45:02,600 --> 00:45:03,867
Or... or wire sir?

1026
00:45:03,867 --> 00:45:07,533
Or wire can be attached
to an object to make it float.

1027
00:45:07,533 --> 00:45:09,033
Oh, like a biscuit?

1028
00:45:09,033 --> 00:45:10,233
We'll get to that.

1029
00:45:10,233 --> 00:45:12,933
Anyway, the object you're going
to be floating has to have

1030
00:45:12,933 --> 00:45:16,333
a high degree
for humor or levity,

1031
00:45:16,333 --> 00:45:17,933
just a little joke
for the TA's there.

1032
00:45:17,933 --> 00:45:21,167
Now, I'm going to be
floating some objects here.

1033
00:45:21,533 --> 00:45:22,800
Uh, with wire?

1034
00:45:22,800 --> 00:45:25,533
With wire, and you're going to
tell me if they're funny or not.

1035
00:45:25,533 --> 00:45:28,700
All right?
First, we start off with a coat.

1036
00:45:28,700 --> 00:45:30,533
Funny or not funny?

1037
00:45:30,533 --> 00:45:32,267
-[Tom] Funny?
-Right, funny.

1038
00:45:32,267 --> 00:45:35,133
Next, Cam-bot, bring it over
to the right here a little bit.

1039
00:45:35,133 --> 00:45:37,367
A wrench, funny or not funny?

1040
00:45:37,733 --> 00:45:39,067
[Tom] Uh, I'd say funny.

1041
00:45:39,067 --> 00:45:40,867
Right, funny, finally.

1042
00:45:40,867 --> 00:45:42,900
-A harmonica.
-Oh, oh, oh, oh!

1043
00:45:42,900 --> 00:45:44,467
Funny or not funny?

1044
00:45:44,467 --> 00:45:46,733
-[Crow] Oh, oh, oh, oh, oh, oh.
-Okay, Crow, Crow.

1045
00:45:46,733 --> 00:45:49,833
Uh, uh... I forgot the question.

1046
00:45:49,833 --> 00:45:51,600
Funny or not funny?

1047
00:45:51,600 --> 00:45:53,733
Uh, funny.

1048
00:45:53,733 --> 00:45:55,033
Are you sure?

1049
00:45:55,033 --> 00:45:56,200
Not funny.

1050
00:45:56,200 --> 00:45:58,467
It was funny.
Stick to your guns, Crow.

1051
00:45:58,467 --> 00:46:00,800
Now I'm going to be
showing you some flashcards.

1052
00:46:00,800 --> 00:46:04,333
You'll be awarded 20 points
for every correct flashcard.

1053
00:46:04,333 --> 00:46:07,733
These are objects that
are funny or not funny

1054
00:46:07,733 --> 00:46:10,200
when they're floating.
All right? Start things off.

1055
00:46:10,200 --> 00:46:12,333
-Sand.
-Uh, not funny.

1056
00:46:12,333 --> 00:46:15,200
Right. Okay. Orange juice,
funny or not funny?

1057
00:46:15,200 --> 00:46:16,733
-Funny.
-Right.

1058
00:46:16,733 --> 00:46:18,033
Next. A baseball?

1059
00:46:18,033 --> 00:46:19,233
Funny.

1060
00:46:19,233 --> 00:46:20,500
Wrong, not funny.

1061
00:46:20,500 --> 00:46:22,967
All right. Woodchuck,
funny or not funny floating?

1062
00:46:22,967 --> 00:46:24,867
-Funny.
-Extremely funny.

1063
00:46:24,867 --> 00:46:27,133
Next, funny
or not funny floating?

1064
00:46:27,133 --> 00:46:28,233
Sally Field.

1065
00:46:28,233 --> 00:46:30,400
-Not funny.
-Right. Next?

1066
00:46:30,400 --> 00:46:32,933
Funny or not funny
floating Sebastian Cabot.

1067
00:46:32,933 --> 00:46:34,000
Funny.

1068
00:46:34,000 --> 00:46:36,367
Right, extremely funny
but impossible.

1069
00:46:36,367 --> 00:46:39,100
Finally, the rules get
a little blurry here, guys.

1070
00:46:39,100 --> 00:46:41,967
Christopher Reeves in
Super Man,
funny or not funny floating?

1071
00:46:41,967 --> 00:46:43,200
-Not funny.
-Right.

1072
00:46:43,200 --> 00:46:45,133
Christopher Reeves in
Monsignor?

1073
00:46:45,133 --> 00:46:46,600
-Funny.
-Right.

1074
00:46:46,600 --> 00:46:49,467
Now, Christopher Reeves kissing
Michael Caine in Death Trap.

1075
00:46:49,467 --> 00:46:52,533
-Oh, funny.
-Right. Finally, Gallagher.

1076
00:46:52,533 --> 00:46:53,967
Funny or not funning floating?

1077
00:46:53,967 --> 00:46:56,233
-Not funny.
-Extremely unfunny.

1078
00:46:56,233 --> 00:46:58,600
Now, we go into
the lightning round.

1079
00:46:58,600 --> 00:47:01,600
Cam-bot, put it up on the screen
and Magic Voice,

1080
00:47:01,600 --> 00:47:03,267
let's have the password.

1081
00:47:03,267 --> 00:47:04,933
[Magic Voice]
The secret password is

1082
00:47:04,933 --> 00:47:06,733
"Things which are
Funny Floating."

1083
00:47:06,733 --> 00:47:07,700
[bell dings]

1084
00:47:07,700 --> 00:47:09,567
Uh, you take your shoe off
with this.

1085
00:47:09,567 --> 00:47:11,767
A piece of cheese,
a come-a-long.

1086
00:47:11,767 --> 00:47:12,867
Shoe horn.

1087
00:47:12,867 --> 00:47:14,167
-Um, right.
-[bell dings]

1088
00:47:14,167 --> 00:47:16,900
Uh... you keep meat in this.

1089
00:47:16,900 --> 00:47:18,167
-Uh, your mouth?
-[bell dings]

1090
00:47:18,167 --> 00:47:21,600
Uh, right.
Um, uh, a juggler.

1091
00:47:21,600 --> 00:47:23,233
-Boring things.
-A juggler.

1092
00:47:23,233 --> 00:47:24,700
-Uh...
-Hackneyed.

1093
00:47:24,700 --> 00:47:26,067
Things at
a Renaissance festival.

1094
00:47:26,067 --> 00:47:27,600
Bad actor.

1095
00:47:27,600 --> 00:47:29,000
Things you say to a Bishop.

1096
00:47:29,000 --> 00:47:30,300
Pass, pass.

1097
00:47:30,300 --> 00:47:32,233
Um, flashing light.

1098
00:47:32,233 --> 00:47:34,200
-Uh, sale at K-Mart.
-[Crow] Flashing light.

1099
00:47:34,200 --> 00:47:35,500
-Buzzer.
-[buzzer sound]

1100
00:47:35,500 --> 00:47:36,533
Times Square at midnight.

1101
00:47:37,233 --> 00:47:38,500
Things that happen
on movie sets.

1102
00:47:38,500 --> 00:47:39,667
-[bell ringing]
-Yeah, that's it.

1103
00:47:39,667 --> 00:47:46,233
-Movie set
-[both] Ahh...

1104
00:47:52,900 --> 00:47:54,967
Something no human being
ever saw before.

1105
00:47:55,767 --> 00:47:57,900
-[Joel] A frogman.
-[Tom] A duck-billed platypus.

1106
00:47:57,900 --> 00:47:59,100
[Crow] Scuba gear.

1107
00:47:59,100 --> 00:48:02,433
[Joel] Uh, no a piece of lint.
It lives in your stomach.

1108
00:48:02,433 --> 00:48:03,933
-[Crow] Lloyd Bridges.
-[Joel] Never mind.

1109
00:48:03,933 --> 00:48:05,367
You know it's funny,

1110
00:48:05,367 --> 00:48:07,400
one never thinks of the Earth
that way,

1111
00:48:08,200 --> 00:48:09,467
as a dying planet

1112
00:48:09,467 --> 00:48:11,533
with nothing to give out
but reflected light.

1113
00:48:11,533 --> 00:48:14,633
[Joel] Oh, that is romantic.
Think I'll go slit my wrists.

1114
00:48:14,633 --> 00:48:16,900
Don't speak so disrespectfully
of the moon.

1115
00:48:16,900 --> 00:48:20,067
[Crow] And whatever you do,
don't say anything about Texas.

1116
00:48:20,467 --> 00:48:22,533
I've done some of my
best work by moonlight.

1117
00:48:22,533 --> 00:48:23,733
[Joel] Boom.

1118
00:48:24,267 --> 00:48:26,000
I saw this light very effective.

1119
00:48:26,367 --> 00:48:27,433
For navigation?

1120
00:48:28,500 --> 00:48:29,833
For any purpose.

1121
00:48:30,967 --> 00:48:32,000
Don't underrate it.

1122
00:48:32,267 --> 00:48:33,633
Moonlight is potent.

1123
00:48:35,200 --> 00:48:37,900
You're speaking of
its effect on men and women?

1124
00:48:37,900 --> 00:48:39,133
-[Joel] No tree frogs.
-On women.

1125
00:48:39,133 --> 00:48:40,733
I'll need to stop myself.

1126
00:48:40,967 --> 00:48:43,933
-Oh. You're immune.
-[Crow] No. I'm Lloyd.

1127
00:48:43,933 --> 00:48:45,100
That's the myoon.

1128
00:48:45,100 --> 00:48:46,267
Do you still need it?

1129
00:48:48,367 --> 00:48:50,100
But did you ever park
in an open convertible

1130
00:48:50,100 --> 00:48:52,000
on the cliffs
overlooking the Pacific?

1131
00:48:52,000 --> 00:48:54,500
[Joel] And then drive off
laughing maniacally?

1132
00:48:54,500 --> 00:48:56,067
[Floyd] ...the moon
hung up there like a lantern.

1133
00:48:56,700 --> 00:48:58,900
The blue light from it
walking across the water,

1134
00:48:59,633 --> 00:49:01,300
radio playing a nice tune.

1135
00:49:01,300 --> 00:49:03,467
-[Tom] Dead body in a trunk.
-Waves wooshing on the beach.

1136
00:49:03,467 --> 00:49:04,800
[Tom] bag of quicklime.

1137
00:49:05,900 --> 00:49:07,167
No, I, uh...

1138
00:49:08,100 --> 00:49:09,667
perhaps unfortunately,

1139
00:49:10,200 --> 00:49:11,733
I never had the time

1140
00:49:11,733 --> 00:49:13,333
-nor the thought...
-[Crow] Or the quick line.

1141
00:49:13,333 --> 00:49:15,500
...to do anything
much beyond my profession.

1142
00:49:16,233 --> 00:49:18,567
I don't think you have never
looked at that old moon

1143
00:49:18,567 --> 00:49:20,567
except for astronomical reasons.

1144
00:49:21,800 --> 00:49:22,833
You're right.

1145
00:49:23,167 --> 00:49:24,300
I have

1146
00:49:25,067 --> 00:49:26,300
in Rome once,

1147
00:49:26,833 --> 00:49:27,933
in Switzerland.

1148
00:49:27,933 --> 00:49:29,600
-[Joel] Rome, Switzerland?
-At Lake Lugano.

1149
00:49:30,400 --> 00:49:32,133
What a nice stroll.

1150
00:49:32,333 --> 00:49:34,400
-Stroll?
-Yes.

1151
00:49:34,400 --> 00:49:36,667
Yes, we walked slowly
around the edge of the lake

1152
00:49:36,667 --> 00:49:38,000
for two hours.

1153
00:49:38,000 --> 00:49:39,200
And never stopped?

1154
00:49:39,200 --> 00:49:40,300
No.

1155
00:49:41,200 --> 00:49:42,733
[Crow German accent]
Then I measured

1156
00:49:42,733 --> 00:49:44,067
the velocity of our walk.

1157
00:49:45,000 --> 00:49:46,600
It was lovely there.

1158
00:49:47,233 --> 00:49:49,567
Water black like cold coffee,

1159
00:49:49,967 --> 00:49:53,233
the moonlight like flecks of
ice cream floating on it.

1160
00:49:53,233 --> 00:49:55,767
[Joel] Parking lot
like a Libbyland dinner.

1161
00:49:55,767 --> 00:49:57,733
[Crow] Quick, somebody get her
a sandwich.

1162
00:49:57,733 --> 00:49:59,467
Oh, but that was so long ago,

1163
00:50:00,000 --> 00:50:01,100
another world.

1164
00:50:01,100 --> 00:50:02,267
[Tom] Yeah, the Earth.

1165
00:50:05,833 --> 00:50:08,633
-Fine scientist I am.
-[Crow] Michael Feinstein.

1166
00:50:08,633 --> 00:50:09,700
Daydream.

1167
00:50:11,067 --> 00:50:12,600
It's good for you
once in a while.

1168
00:50:14,067 --> 00:50:15,833
[Crow] It's wonderful.

1169
00:50:15,833 --> 00:50:17,667
I supposed it is, but...

1170
00:50:19,533 --> 00:50:20,800
Not now.

1171
00:50:20,800 --> 00:50:22,333
[Tom] I'm going to be sick now.

1172
00:50:23,967 --> 00:50:25,433
I still say it.

1173
00:50:26,167 --> 00:50:28,167
-Say what?
-[all] It.

1174
00:50:28,167 --> 00:50:29,800
That moonlight's
effective stuff.

1175
00:50:31,267 --> 00:50:32,533
You're quite right.

1176
00:50:33,567 --> 00:50:35,667
[Joel] Where do you
want to be in two years?

1177
00:50:40,833 --> 00:50:41,900
[Joel] If only...

1178
00:50:41,900 --> 00:50:43,700
What effect a double dose
like this

1179
00:50:43,700 --> 00:50:45,167
would have in a convertible?

1180
00:50:45,167 --> 00:50:48,100
[Crow] Would it rust, crumble?
Have to put it up on blocks.

1181
00:50:48,100 --> 00:50:49,167
Skip it.

1182
00:50:50,300 --> 00:50:51,633
[explosion]

1183
00:50:52,033 --> 00:50:54,167
-Meteorites!
-[Joel] Big ones, too!

1184
00:50:54,167 --> 00:50:55,567
-Screamers!
-[Crow] Wow.

1185
00:50:56,300 --> 00:50:59,267
[Joel] Hey, cool. That one
looks like my gallstone.

1186
00:50:59,267 --> 00:51:01,467
[Crow] Looks like
buttery caramel corn.

1187
00:51:01,467 --> 00:51:03,467
Please visit our snack bar.

1188
00:51:03,467 --> 00:51:04,900
[Tom] I'll have a Sprite,
please.

1189
00:51:04,900 --> 00:51:07,733
-Thanks, Mr. Crow.
-[Crow] Way to go, kid.

1190
00:51:16,033 --> 00:51:19,467
[Crow whistling]

1191
00:51:24,600 --> 00:51:26,367
[Crow] Looks like poppy cock.

1192
00:51:29,167 --> 00:51:31,700
[Tom] Well, who needs a drink?
I know I do.

1193
00:51:32,133 --> 00:51:33,167
Boy...

1194
00:51:34,133 --> 00:51:36,300
I've been through some
pretty heavy flack in my day,

1195
00:51:36,300 --> 00:51:38,633
but that's the worst
I've ever had thrown at me.

1196
00:51:38,633 --> 00:51:40,733
[Crow] Yeah, yeah.
We know, in Texas.

1197
00:51:42,667 --> 00:51:43,800
Say...

1198
00:51:44,133 --> 00:51:45,767
[Crow] That could be a musical.

1199
00:51:46,400 --> 00:51:49,267
Maybe somebody don't want us
to get where we aim to get.

1200
00:51:49,267 --> 00:51:51,267
[Joel] Yeah, the God of grammar.

1201
00:51:54,533 --> 00:51:56,233
Oh, cheer up, Harry.

1202
00:51:56,667 --> 00:51:59,133
After what we've been through,
we'll get there all right.

1203
00:52:01,733 --> 00:52:04,267
Maybe it'd have been better if
one of them had struck the ship.

1204
00:52:05,367 --> 00:52:06,667
At least it would
have been sudden.

1205
00:52:06,667 --> 00:52:07,833
[Tom]
Probably wrinkle my shirt.

1206
00:52:07,833 --> 00:52:08,833
Quick.

1207
00:52:09,567 --> 00:52:11,667
[Crow] Oh, are you
gonna cry now, baby?

1208
00:52:16,167 --> 00:52:18,267
-Now, your computation?
-[Joel] Dog ate 'em.

1209
00:52:18,267 --> 00:52:20,933
We have to add
12 percent 03 to A16.

1210
00:52:20,933 --> 00:52:24,233
Right. 12 percent 03 to A16.

1211
00:52:24,233 --> 00:52:26,667
This means we'll have to
rearrange some of the fuel tanks

1212
00:52:26,667 --> 00:52:28,133
and all the connections.

1213
00:52:28,133 --> 00:52:30,600
[Joel] It would take a rocket
scientist to explain this.

1214
00:52:30,600 --> 00:52:32,333
We'll replace these H tanks.

1215
00:52:32,333 --> 00:52:34,233
[Crow] And drop
some of the bridesmaids

1216
00:52:34,233 --> 00:52:36,533
and order a smaller cake.
Happy now?

1217
00:52:39,667 --> 00:52:41,233
Think you can manage
a half ton tank?

1218
00:52:41,233 --> 00:52:42,433
Oh, sure.

1219
00:52:42,700 --> 00:52:45,300
Down on my ranch, used to throw
a little old thousand pound

1220
00:52:45,300 --> 00:52:47,067
steer over my shoulder
every morning.

1221
00:52:47,067 --> 00:52:49,233
-[Tom] Then I married her.
-Little bull you were throwing.

1222
00:52:49,233 --> 00:52:51,700
[Tom] Get down on the toilet,
you chromo.

1223
00:52:52,700 --> 00:52:53,800
Sheesh.

1224
00:52:56,033 --> 00:52:59,500
[music playing]

1225
00:53:02,600 --> 00:53:04,733
[Joel underwater]
Now let me get this straight.

1226
00:53:04,733 --> 00:53:09,567
We go down here and just stare
at stuff again until we fix it.

1227
00:53:09,567 --> 00:53:10,900
Is that right?

1228
00:53:10,900 --> 00:53:12,300
[Tom underwater] Yep.

1229
00:53:15,700 --> 00:53:17,900
[Crow wiseguy voice] That's
blinker light fluid all right.

1230
00:53:17,900 --> 00:53:20,000
Want I should check
your muffler belt, too?

1231
00:53:20,000 --> 00:53:21,700
All right, Sampson.
Let's see you do your stuff.

1232
00:53:21,700 --> 00:53:23,400
Think I was kidding, huh?

1233
00:53:25,300 --> 00:53:27,667
-[Tom] Hmm.
-You'll never prove it here.

1234
00:53:31,633 --> 00:53:33,600
[Joel] What's a Greek column
doing down here?

1235
00:53:33,600 --> 00:53:34,633
Doctor...

1236
00:53:35,467 --> 00:53:37,933
can we be quite sure
these proportions are safe?

1237
00:53:38,633 --> 00:53:40,567
We never proved them
by experiment.

1238
00:53:41,067 --> 00:53:43,400
The mathematical theory
is beyond question.

1239
00:53:44,700 --> 00:53:46,133
03 though.

1240
00:53:46,467 --> 00:53:48,900
Sometimes it behaves
unpredictably.

1241
00:53:49,433 --> 00:53:50,767
Woman's intuition again.

1242
00:53:50,767 --> 00:53:53,900
[Joel] Oh, there he goes with
the woman thing again. Great.

1243
00:53:58,400 --> 00:54:00,167
Twelve percent 03 to A16.

1244
00:54:00,167 --> 00:54:01,933
Twelve percent 03 to A16.

1245
00:54:01,933 --> 00:54:03,167
[Tom] You sunk my battleship!

1246
00:54:03,167 --> 00:54:05,267
Thirty-two degrees.
Present position is...

1247
00:54:05,267 --> 00:54:08,067
We'll correct course when
we're in pilot flight once more.

1248
00:54:08,067 --> 00:54:09,267
All right.

1249
00:54:09,267 --> 00:54:10,900
-All ready?
-Ready.

1250
00:54:12,800 --> 00:54:13,900
Stand by, everybody.

1251
00:54:13,900 --> 00:54:15,000
[Crow] It's going to get nuts.

1252
00:54:15,000 --> 00:54:16,400
[Joel] Nice pose.

1253
00:54:18,267 --> 00:54:19,533
[noise]

1254
00:54:20,200 --> 00:54:21,433
[Joel] Well, she's happy.

1255
00:54:23,200 --> 00:54:25,100
[Tom] Got to... hang on...

1256
00:54:25,467 --> 00:54:27,933
[Crow] Mrs. Carmichael,
this is your fault.

1257
00:54:28,133 --> 00:54:31,600
Oh, Rickyyy...

1258
00:54:31,800 --> 00:54:33,667
[Tom] Must... hang... on.

1259
00:54:38,067 --> 00:54:40,600
[Joel] Uh, excuse me.
This is my stop.

1260
00:54:40,600 --> 00:54:42,467
[suspenseful music playing]

1261
00:54:45,667 --> 00:54:47,800
[Joel] That went
rather well I think.

1262
00:54:47,800 --> 00:54:50,033
[Crow] I did it.
I'm Mr. Air Speed.

1263
00:54:50,033 --> 00:54:53,133
Next needle nose.
We did it all.

1264
00:54:53,767 --> 00:54:55,900
Will you repeat that again,
Dr. Hurley?

1265
00:54:55,900 --> 00:54:57,467
[Joel] You need one of those
inflatable donuts.

1266
00:54:57,467 --> 00:54:58,733
-Are you certain?
-[Joel] Oh, yeah.

1267
00:54:58,733 --> 00:55:00,067
No solid food either.

1268
00:55:01,233 --> 00:55:03,867
-Scratching irritates it though.
-I see. Thank you.

1269
00:55:03,867 --> 00:55:05,433
[Joel] Well, it could be worse.

1270
00:55:08,533 --> 00:55:10,267
They are completely
off their course.

1271
00:55:10,267 --> 00:55:13,100
[Joel] Those guys could at least
stop shopping at the same store.

1272
00:55:13,100 --> 00:55:15,533
Moving with incredible velocity

1273
00:55:16,667 --> 00:55:18,867
out into limitless space.

1274
00:55:19,333 --> 00:55:20,767
[Crow] Heavy.

1275
00:55:22,467 --> 00:55:24,000
I can't understand it.

1276
00:55:24,500 --> 00:55:27,200
[Joel] Come on, scatter, guys.
One bullet could get all of us.

1277
00:55:36,500 --> 00:55:38,967
[Tom] All right.
Who made the Kool-Aid?

1278
00:55:39,800 --> 00:55:42,467
[Joel] Love was
cooling in the mist.

1279
00:55:43,100 --> 00:55:45,000
[Tom] It’s 7:15 in deep space,

1280
00:55:45,000 --> 00:55:47,000
we’re still on the business end
of a beautiful day.

1281
00:55:47,000 --> 00:55:48,300
Got some Bruce Hornsby
and the Range

1282
00:55:48,300 --> 00:55:50,600
and then three in a row
from Paula Abdul

1283
00:55:51,367 --> 00:55:52,933
[Crow] She awakes
with a hairball

1284
00:55:52,933 --> 00:55:54,833
and the worst breath
of the day.

1285
00:55:56,600 --> 00:55:58,400
-[Joel] Oh.
-[Crow] Eat an apple.

1286
00:56:00,533 --> 00:56:03,900
[Joel] Oh, I'm late honey.
Could you drive me to work?

1287
00:56:03,900 --> 00:56:05,767
Oh, we're in space.

1288
00:56:13,300 --> 00:56:15,633
[Tom] Better grab
a Hefty bag and start at it.

1289
00:56:16,033 --> 00:56:17,233
I'm all right.

1290
00:56:19,267 --> 00:56:21,600
[Crow] You know,
one time this happened to me.

1291
00:56:21,600 --> 00:56:23,633
I was in a convertible and I...

1292
00:56:24,500 --> 00:56:25,900
We're not getting enough oxygen.

1293
00:56:27,933 --> 00:56:30,300
[Crow] My lungs are...
well, you know.

1294
00:56:30,800 --> 00:56:32,500
[Van Horn] Bill? Bill?

1295
00:56:36,033 --> 00:56:37,367
[Joel] Open the window.

1296
00:56:37,367 --> 00:56:38,967
[Tom] That was
the worst party ever.

1297
00:56:38,967 --> 00:56:40,900
Who invited the Theta Chis?

1298
00:56:40,900 --> 00:56:41,900
Dr. Eckstrom.

1299
00:56:42,833 --> 00:56:43,933
Dr. Eckstrom.

1300
00:56:43,933 --> 00:56:45,267
Dr. Eckstrom.

1301
00:56:45,267 --> 00:56:47,667
[Joel] Wake up old guy.
Gale Gordon.

1302
00:56:47,667 --> 00:56:48,733
[Crow] Oh, quick, here.

1303
00:56:48,733 --> 00:56:50,333
You move his mouth.
I'll do the voice.

1304
00:56:50,333 --> 00:56:52,033
Say hello to the folks, Bobby.

1305
00:56:52,033 --> 00:56:53,333
[Tom] Hello, folks.

1306
00:56:54,200 --> 00:56:55,667
How long has it been since...

1307
00:56:55,667 --> 00:56:57,600
[Joel] Hey,
that's rather personal.

1308
00:56:58,567 --> 00:56:59,733
How are the others?

1309
00:57:00,833 --> 00:57:02,833
[Van Horn] I think everybody's
going to be all right.

1310
00:57:04,800 --> 00:57:07,333
-[Crow] Nice stash.
-It must have been days.

1311
00:57:07,900 --> 00:57:09,800
Fortunately,
the engines were turned off.

1312
00:57:09,800 --> 00:57:10,833
When?

1313
00:57:11,400 --> 00:57:14,267
I must have turned the levers
off when I blacked out.

1314
00:57:14,600 --> 00:57:15,867
But what speed?

1315
00:57:16,233 --> 00:57:18,000
You know the consequences
of a body moving

1316
00:57:18,000 --> 00:57:20,600
with unchecked velocity
in free space.

1317
00:57:21,333 --> 00:57:23,433
[Crow]
Uh, we're our own grandparents?

1318
00:57:24,800 --> 00:57:26,333
So we made a little detour.

1319
00:57:26,933 --> 00:57:28,967
Now we got to double back
that last road sign.

1320
00:57:28,967 --> 00:57:30,033
Come on.
Let's go to work.

1321
00:57:30,033 --> 00:57:32,533
[Joel] Uh, this is a spaceship,
Tom, not a car.

1322
00:57:32,533 --> 00:57:33,933
Know what I'm saying?

1323
00:57:33,933 --> 00:57:35,633
Remember that blue thing?
Good.

1324
00:57:35,833 --> 00:57:38,300
What do you know that's bad
about the big blue thing?

1325
00:57:38,300 --> 00:57:40,267
We want to be on it. Dig?

1326
00:57:41,433 --> 00:57:44,267
Can't be. The instruments
must have gone crazy.

1327
00:57:44,500 --> 00:57:46,533
[Tom] They're
certifiably insane. [chuckles]

1328
00:57:46,533 --> 00:57:47,733
I don't think so.

1329
00:57:48,733 --> 00:57:50,033
By heading into space,

1330
00:57:50,033 --> 00:57:52,500
we've added the Earth's
orbital velocity to our own.

1331
00:57:52,500 --> 00:57:53,900
[Crow] And that's bad, right?

1332
00:57:53,900 --> 00:57:55,100
We're really moving.

1333
00:57:55,567 --> 00:57:57,400
I should say
at least several times

1334
00:57:57,400 --> 00:57:59,333
the maximum speed
of your indicator.

1335
00:57:59,333 --> 00:58:02,033
[Joel] Not to mention it smells
like a bus station in here.

1336
00:58:02,033 --> 00:58:04,333
At least have an approximation
of our position very shortly.

1337
00:58:04,833 --> 00:58:05,900
Fuel consumption?

1338
00:58:05,900 --> 00:58:07,400
-[Tom] No thanks. I've had...
-Just a moment.

1339
00:58:10,800 --> 00:58:12,300
-Doctor?
-What?

1340
00:58:12,700 --> 00:58:14,633
I cut in resistances
to raise the scale.

1341
00:58:14,833 --> 00:58:15,867
But look.

1342
00:58:16,367 --> 00:58:19,033
[Crow] That's very pretty,
Steve, but what does it mean?

1343
00:58:19,600 --> 00:58:20,700
[Dr. Eckstrom] Acceleration.

1344
00:58:21,567 --> 00:58:23,133
Definite acceleration but...

1345
00:58:24,033 --> 00:58:26,367
with the motor's off,
that's impossible unless...

1346
00:58:26,600 --> 00:58:28,567
-[Bill] This can't be correct.
-What?

1347
00:58:28,900 --> 00:58:30,833
I seem to be getting
a strong reflection of impulse

1348
00:58:30,833 --> 00:58:32,800
directly ahead 50,000 miles.

1349
00:58:33,867 --> 00:58:36,467
-That explains it.
-Explains what?

1350
00:58:38,167 --> 00:58:39,967
[Tom] We've been
looking in a mirror.

1351
00:58:42,533 --> 00:58:46,100
I've got to touch all of you.
Duck, duck, duck, goose.

1352
00:58:46,433 --> 00:58:47,500
Run.

1353
00:58:49,967 --> 00:58:51,200
[Dr. Eckstrom] Unbelievable.

1354
00:58:53,533 --> 00:58:55,233
It couldn't be mere chance.

1355
00:58:55,567 --> 00:58:57,767
[Joel] Uh-huh, uh-huh.
Keep talking.

1356
00:58:59,033 --> 00:59:00,167
-[Joel] Out with it.
-[Tom] Yeah.

1357
00:59:00,167 --> 00:59:01,233
[Joel] Out with it.

1358
00:59:01,233 --> 00:59:03,300
I don't quite know
how to tell you it's so...

1359
00:59:03,300 --> 00:59:05,200
[Joel] Uh-huh.
Spit it out, Brad.

1360
00:59:05,200 --> 00:59:07,067
If I could have even
dreamed that it...

1361
00:59:07,067 --> 00:59:08,133
[Crow] Cut to the chase.

1362
00:59:08,133 --> 00:59:09,400
An incredible
set of circumstances...

1363
00:59:09,400 --> 00:59:11,100
-[Joel] Get on with it.
-Precisely at exactly...

1364
00:59:11,100 --> 00:59:12,667
[Joel] We're listening.
We're here, hey.

1365
00:59:12,667 --> 00:59:14,533
...would carry us unerringly
through space

1366
00:59:14,533 --> 00:59:17,367
to our most congenial
planetary neighbor Mars.

1367
00:59:17,367 --> 00:59:19,333
-[Joel] We're with you.
-Mars?

1368
00:59:20,600 --> 00:59:22,033
[Crow] As in the candy bar?

1369
00:59:22,633 --> 00:59:24,600
[Tom] Wow.
Looks just like Art Carney.

1370
00:59:24,600 --> 00:59:26,233
[Van Horn] Utterly unbelievable.

1371
00:59:26,233 --> 00:59:28,300
[Tom] I suppose they have
a bigger Mars than Texas, huh?

1372
00:59:28,300 --> 00:59:30,233
Mars. What do you know?

1373
00:59:30,233 --> 00:59:31,500
[Crow]
Well, of all the dumb luck.

1374
00:59:31,500 --> 00:59:33,367
I'm sure, no way, Mars.

1375
00:59:33,367 --> 00:59:37,700
Here we are in endless space and
you park right in front of Mars.

1376
00:59:37,700 --> 00:59:39,667
We must pause
and observe respectfully

1377
00:59:39,667 --> 00:59:41,833
while something infinitely
greater assumes control.

1378
00:59:41,833 --> 00:59:42,933
[Joel] Heavy.

1379
00:59:42,933 --> 00:59:45,033
I believe
this is one of those times.

1380
00:59:45,467 --> 00:59:47,100
We should go on, of course.

1381
00:59:47,833 --> 00:59:49,700
Our overall
fuel consumption was...

1382
00:59:49,700 --> 00:59:50,767
Forty-two percent.

1383
00:59:51,033 --> 00:59:52,167
[Joel] And that's good.

1384
00:59:52,167 --> 00:59:54,200
A Martian landing, approaching
a planet with atmosphere

1385
00:59:54,200 --> 00:59:56,200
is far easier
than the lunar maneuver.

1386
00:59:56,733 --> 00:59:58,500
[Joel] And that's good.

1387
00:59:58,500 --> 01:00:00,567
We should be betraying
an unprecedented opportunity

1388
01:00:00,567 --> 01:00:01,733
to do otherwise.

1389
01:00:02,500 --> 01:00:05,133
Then I suggest there's a couple
of little things we might do.

1390
01:00:05,133 --> 01:00:06,700
The ship isn't going
to land itself, you know.

1391
01:00:06,700 --> 01:00:09,067
-[Joel] And that's bad.
-[Tom] What's he mean by that?

1392
01:00:09,433 --> 01:00:10,733
[Crow] Splat.

1393
01:00:10,733 --> 01:00:12,167
Mars. [chuckles]

1394
01:00:12,533 --> 01:00:13,967
-I'll be darned.
-[Tom] What do you know.

1395
01:00:13,967 --> 01:00:15,100
We must first compute

1396
01:00:15,100 --> 01:00:16,433
the correct
thrust and fuel mixture

1397
01:00:16,433 --> 01:00:18,133
to comply with
anticipated conditions.

1398
01:00:18,133 --> 01:00:19,667
[Joel] And then we dream.

1399
01:00:19,667 --> 01:00:22,000
[Van Horn] ...twelve thousand
five hundred miles an hour.

1400
01:00:22,000 --> 01:00:24,400
Oxygen at surface level,
15 percent.

1401
01:00:24,400 --> 01:00:25,867
Prepare to change
the parallel course

1402
01:00:25,867 --> 01:00:27,933
-at distance 1,500 miles.
-[Joel] Check.

1403
01:00:28,133 --> 01:00:30,100
[Crow] And wipe that
dopey grin off your face.

1404
01:00:30,100 --> 01:00:31,167
I've located it.

1405
01:00:31,167 --> 01:00:33,233
-Located what?
-[Tom] Our clean shorts.

1406
01:00:33,833 --> 01:00:35,067
Well, where are they?

1407
01:00:35,067 --> 01:00:36,433
Here. Take a look.

1408
01:00:38,467 --> 01:00:41,467
[Joel] Hey, Tex, what do you say
we blast 'em just for fun?

1409
01:00:41,467 --> 01:00:42,633
[Tom chuckles]

1410
01:00:42,633 --> 01:00:43,867
For old times’ sake.

1411
01:00:43,867 --> 01:00:45,233
[William] What do you know
about that?

1412
01:00:45,233 --> 01:00:46,767
[Tom] Well, I know
the little one's the moon.

1413
01:00:46,767 --> 01:00:48,567
Is it all right to call Texas
a mere speck now?

1414
01:00:49,733 --> 01:00:51,533
[Joel]
Man, I never liked that guy.

1415
01:00:51,533 --> 01:00:54,067
[Crow] Now Linus is different.
His hair is kind of crazy.

1416
01:00:54,067 --> 01:00:55,600
You draw 'em like this.

1417
01:00:56,167 --> 01:01:00,567
At 50 miles altitude, we will
execute a turn of 90 degrees,

1418
01:01:00,567 --> 01:01:02,800
bringing the rocket
into the landing position.

1419
01:01:02,800 --> 01:01:04,667
[Joel] Could you sharpen this?

1420
01:01:04,667 --> 01:01:06,467
Since the gravitational
attraction to Mars

1421
01:01:06,467 --> 01:01:08,700
is only half as powerful
as that of Earth,

1422
01:01:08,700 --> 01:01:11,433
a thrust of 1200 tons will be
sufficient to make the landing.

1423
01:01:11,433 --> 01:01:14,400
-Fuel?
-Hydrogen and oxygen plus A12.

1424
01:01:14,400 --> 01:01:15,767
[Tom] That's my favorite blend.

1425
01:01:15,767 --> 01:01:17,533
Water, shaken not stirred.

1426
01:01:18,567 --> 01:01:20,467
-[Joel inhales]
-One hundred seventy miles.

1427
01:01:20,467 --> 01:01:21,867
[Tom] He's getting a facial.

1428
01:01:21,867 --> 01:01:23,300
[Crow] His pores are opening.

1429
01:01:23,300 --> 01:01:24,467
[Harry] Sixty-five.

1430
01:01:26,800 --> 01:01:28,800
[Joel] Oh, that one
looks kind of like a horsey.

1431
01:01:28,800 --> 01:01:30,600
Hey, that one looks like
our friend, Dana.

1432
01:01:30,600 --> 01:01:32,033
-Sixty.
-[Joel] Yeah, I can see too.

1433
01:01:32,033 --> 01:01:34,333
Looks like Dana
and I see the horsey. It's neat.

1434
01:01:34,333 --> 01:01:35,433
[Dr. Eckstrom] Stand by to turn.

1435
01:01:36,033 --> 01:01:37,033
Fifty.

1436
01:01:37,667 --> 01:01:38,733
Turn.

1437
01:01:38,733 --> 01:01:40,667
[Crow] Coming about. Hard alee.

1438
01:01:40,667 --> 01:01:42,967
Watch the boom.
I see the great white.

1439
01:01:43,333 --> 01:01:44,533
[Tom] Queequeg.

1440
01:01:44,533 --> 01:01:45,833
[buzzing]

1441
01:01:45,833 --> 01:01:47,600
[Joel]
They're in the spin cycle.

1442
01:01:47,600 --> 01:01:50,700
Must add fabric softener.

1443
01:01:51,367 --> 01:01:53,667
Socks are sticking to the side.

1444
01:01:56,500 --> 01:01:58,133
[Crow coughing]

1445
01:01:58,133 --> 01:01:59,833
[Crow] Hey,
we're half a bubble off plumb.

1446
01:01:59,833 --> 01:02:01,100
[Tom] Whoa.

1447
01:02:04,267 --> 01:02:05,533
[Joel exhales in relief]

1448
01:02:05,533 --> 01:02:06,767
Ninety-degree turn completed.

1449
01:02:07,233 --> 01:02:08,633
Reduce power.

1450
01:02:10,600 --> 01:02:12,533
[Crow] You know,
once I was on a Tilt A Whirl.

1451
01:02:12,533 --> 01:02:13,800
[Joel, Tom] Shut up.

1452
01:02:14,300 --> 01:02:15,500
[Crow] Goodnight.

1453
01:02:19,800 --> 01:02:23,100
[Joel] Reverse stock footage
come in for landing mode.

1454
01:02:35,000 --> 01:02:36,467
Altitude 40 miles,

1455
01:02:36,933 --> 01:02:38,000
38 miles.

1456
01:02:38,000 --> 01:02:39,567
I only hope
the light holds out.

1457
01:02:39,567 --> 01:02:41,233
It's getting dark down below.

1458
01:02:43,667 --> 01:02:45,533
[Tom]
My chin looks great in here.

1459
01:02:49,133 --> 01:02:50,200
[Joel] Love me.

1460
01:02:50,600 --> 01:02:53,267
Prepare to increase thrust
at an altitude 15 miles.

1461
01:02:53,267 --> 01:02:55,433
[Crow] We'll park it
by the Chrysler.

1462
01:02:56,567 --> 01:02:58,767
[thrusting sound]

1463
01:03:05,167 --> 01:03:06,633
It's getting darker rapidly.

1464
01:03:06,933 --> 01:03:09,400
As far as I can make out, the
terrain is suitable for landing.

1465
01:03:09,400 --> 01:03:11,700
[Tom] It's a good thing
because we're landing.

1466
01:03:12,200 --> 01:03:13,333
Five miles,

1467
01:03:14,233 --> 01:03:16,800
20,000 feet, 18,000.

1468
01:03:16,800 --> 01:03:18,467
Thrust at 1,250 tons.

1469
01:03:19,200 --> 01:03:20,467
Standby to increase power.

1470
01:03:22,833 --> 01:03:23,833
Fifteen thousand.

1471
01:03:23,833 --> 01:03:25,033
[Crow] Bottles of beer
on the wall.

1472
01:03:25,033 --> 01:03:26,900
-[Harry] Twelve thousand.
-Bottles of beer on the wall.

1473
01:03:26,900 --> 01:03:28,667
-[Harry] Ten thousand.
-Bottles of beer on the wall.

1474
01:03:28,667 --> 01:03:29,867
Eight thousand, 7000, 6000.

1475
01:03:29,867 --> 01:03:31,600
We're losing altitude too fast.

1476
01:03:31,600 --> 01:03:33,500
Increase thrust at 3,000 tons.

1477
01:03:33,500 --> 01:03:36,400
[Joel] Uh, that's this thing,
right? Got it.

1478
01:03:36,400 --> 01:03:37,867
Four thousand feet.

1479
01:03:40,333 --> 01:03:41,533
Three thousand.

1480
01:03:43,300 --> 01:03:44,700
[Bill] Two thousand feet.

1481
01:03:44,700 --> 01:03:47,733
Reduce thrust slowly
to 1,250 tons.

1482
01:03:50,267 --> 01:03:51,967
[Tom] Third floor,
notions, ready to wear.

1483
01:03:51,967 --> 01:03:53,067
[Bill] One thousand.

1484
01:03:55,367 --> 01:03:56,433
Five hundred.

1485
01:04:00,200 --> 01:04:02,500
[Joel] Oh, look.
There's a gantry on space.

1486
01:04:03,633 --> 01:04:05,000
[Tom] First floor.

1487
01:04:05,533 --> 01:04:07,167
[Crow]
Like they don't even care.

1488
01:04:08,733 --> 01:04:10,467
Nice work, Floyd.

1489
01:04:10,467 --> 01:04:11,967
[Crow] Oh, thank you, Andy.

1490
01:04:11,967 --> 01:04:14,467
Ooh, what a fine landing. Yes.

1491
01:04:15,700 --> 01:04:17,167
[thunder]

1492
01:04:18,000 --> 01:04:20,067
Mars extending us a welcome.

1493
01:04:20,400 --> 01:04:22,167
[Tom German accent]
Extending us a welcome.

1494
01:04:22,167 --> 01:04:24,500
We'll prepare to disembark
first thing in the morning.

1495
01:04:24,500 --> 01:04:27,167
In the meantime, we can rest
and get our equipment ready.

1496
01:04:27,167 --> 01:04:29,133
Well, that's what
I've been waiting for.

1497
01:04:30,233 --> 01:04:31,333
[Tom] I love equipment.

1498
01:04:34,033 --> 01:04:36,467
I've been itching
to get into one of these things.

1499
01:04:36,467 --> 01:04:38,567
-[Tom] It's my grandma's skin.
-Sorry to disappoint you, Bill.

1500
01:04:38,567 --> 01:04:39,933
We have atmosphere here.

1501
01:04:39,933 --> 01:04:42,167
-We won't need pressure suits.
-[Tom] Oh, man.

1502
01:04:42,167 --> 01:04:43,967
Which works greatly
in our favor, too.

1503
01:04:43,967 --> 01:04:45,233
[Tom] I don't get
to have any fun.

1504
01:04:45,233 --> 01:04:47,267
We can accomplish
so much more unencumbered.

1505
01:04:47,733 --> 01:04:50,633
As far as equipment's concerned,
we'll take oxygen masks,

1506
01:04:50,633 --> 01:04:51,700
camera, paraphernalia.

1507
01:04:51,700 --> 01:04:53,167
What about data
for the return trip?

1508
01:04:53,167 --> 01:04:56,167
[Crow] Yes, yes.
Data for the return trip too.

1509
01:04:56,567 --> 01:04:58,300
Yes, of course. You're right.

1510
01:04:58,767 --> 01:05:00,400
We have so little time here.

1511
01:05:00,400 --> 01:05:01,967
Such an opportunity.

1512
01:05:02,633 --> 01:05:05,000
Let's think about that
for a little while.

1513
01:05:05,233 --> 01:05:06,867
[Joel] Let's turn in
for the night...

1514
01:05:07,833 --> 01:05:09,100
and full day.

1515
01:05:09,100 --> 01:05:12,867
[theme music playing]

1516
01:05:15,100 --> 01:05:17,300
[Joel] Come on
and march in double time.

1517
01:05:17,300 --> 01:05:19,333
[Tom] Come on
and march in double time.

1518
01:05:19,333 --> 01:05:21,867
[Joel] Let's sing a song
by Michael Feinstein.

1519
01:05:21,867 --> 01:05:24,000
[Tom] Sing a song
by Michael Feinstein.

1520
01:05:24,000 --> 01:05:26,133
-[Joel] Sound off.
-[Tom] One, two.

1521
01:05:26,133 --> 01:05:27,267
[Joel] Sound off.

1522
01:05:27,267 --> 01:05:30,800
[all] Three, four,
clap on, clap off,

1523
01:05:30,800 --> 01:05:32,100
the clapper.

1524
01:05:32,100 --> 01:05:35,167
[suspenseful music playing]

1525
01:05:35,167 --> 01:05:37,100
[Crow] We thought
it would be funny

1526
01:05:37,100 --> 01:05:41,367
if we blackened
the eyepieces on his binoculars.

1527
01:05:41,367 --> 01:05:44,200
Let's see what
Walt Disney thinks.

1528
01:05:50,500 --> 01:05:51,800
Look at
the rock formation there.

1529
01:05:51,800 --> 01:05:53,800
[Joel] Looks like Arizona to me.

1530
01:05:54,067 --> 01:05:57,000
-Notice the abundant coloration?
-[Crow] Just more the same.

1531
01:05:57,000 --> 01:05:58,367
...yellow, black.

1532
01:05:58,733 --> 01:06:01,500
[Joel] Raspberry red,
lemon yellow, orange orange.

1533
01:06:02,433 --> 01:06:05,600
Manganese, copper, nickel,
pitch blend.

1534
01:06:05,600 --> 01:06:07,567
[Tom] Sphagnum,
zircon, molybdenum,

1535
01:06:07,567 --> 01:06:09,967
sternum, aquarium, colitis.

1536
01:06:09,967 --> 01:06:12,433
[man in the middle] Are you
staking out a claim here?

1537
01:06:12,433 --> 01:06:14,633
Just making sure
we can find our way back.

1538
01:06:14,633 --> 01:06:16,067
[Joel] You talking to me?

1539
01:06:19,433 --> 01:06:22,067
[Tom] My hose
goes right into my sternum.

1540
01:06:26,367 --> 01:06:29,100
[Joel]
Uh, who brought the sandwiches.

1541
01:06:29,100 --> 01:06:31,167
[Crow] Uh, sandwiches?

1542
01:06:32,333 --> 01:06:37,400
[Tom, Crow, Joel] ♪ Valderi,
Valdera, Valderi, Valdera ♪

1543
01:06:37,400 --> 01:06:39,800
[Tom] Ahh, Ricky.

1544
01:06:50,067 --> 01:06:53,100
[Tom, Crow, Joel humming a song]

1545
01:06:54,667 --> 01:06:56,700
[Crow] Okay, if we don't find
a bush or a tree

1546
01:06:56,700 --> 01:06:59,800
in the next two miles,
well, just go anywhere.

1547
01:07:03,167 --> 01:07:08,700
[Tom, Crow, Joel] ♪ Valderi,
Valdera, Valderi, Valdera ♪

1548
01:07:09,633 --> 01:07:11,367
[Tom chuckles]

1549
01:07:12,533 --> 01:07:16,167
[Tom, Crow, Joel spitting]

1550
01:07:26,500 --> 01:07:31,067
All we've seen so far is
rock and sand, sand and rocks.

1551
01:07:31,067 --> 01:07:32,333
[Joel] You said it.

1552
01:07:32,333 --> 01:07:35,000
I wonder when we're going to run
across some of these Martians.

1553
01:07:35,733 --> 01:07:38,533
From what I read,
they've got pale faces

1554
01:07:38,533 --> 01:07:40,833
and pinheads and fishy eyes.

1555
01:07:40,833 --> 01:07:42,933
[Crow] I heard that
said about Texans too.

1556
01:07:45,833 --> 01:07:46,833
[Tom] What did he say?

1557
01:07:47,133 --> 01:07:49,433
Ah, forget it.
Let's go collect some maggots.

1558
01:07:52,000 --> 01:07:54,300
[Joel] Things on Mars
are pretty bad.

1559
01:07:54,300 --> 01:07:56,333
[Tom] Things on Mars
are pretty bad.

1560
01:07:56,333 --> 01:07:58,800
I'm starting to like
the Rockford Dad.

1561
01:07:58,800 --> 01:08:01,033
[Crow] Starting to like
the Rockford Dad.

1562
01:08:03,100 --> 01:08:06,167
[Joel] This must be
the creamy nugget center...

1563
01:08:06,700 --> 01:08:07,833
of a Mars.

1564
01:08:11,633 --> 01:08:13,033
[Joel] Hey look, guys.

1565
01:08:13,300 --> 01:08:14,867
There's the Statue of Liberty.

1566
01:08:14,867 --> 01:08:17,933
And there's James Franciscus
and Charlton Heston.

1567
01:08:18,233 --> 01:08:20,333
You did it. You finally did it.

1568
01:08:20,333 --> 01:08:22,733
Damn you all to hell.

1569
01:08:23,200 --> 01:08:25,767
[Tom] Hey, look at
all the driftwood.

1570
01:08:25,767 --> 01:08:28,233
We could make
cool stuff out of this.

1571
01:08:32,700 --> 01:08:34,867
[Crow] Gosh, those Stuckys
are everywhere.

1572
01:08:34,867 --> 01:08:37,200
[Joel] All right.
Who wants a nut log anybody?

1573
01:08:37,567 --> 01:08:39,133
I'm going to get
some roasted chicken.

1574
01:08:39,133 --> 01:08:40,400
[Tom] They got onion rings here?

1575
01:08:40,400 --> 01:08:42,367
-[Joel] He wants onion rings.
-[Tom] Get me a loaf for myself.

1576
01:08:42,367 --> 01:08:43,500
[Crow] I want a toy.

1577
01:08:55,767 --> 01:08:58,433
Hey, what kind of lens
you got in that camera there?

1578
01:09:03,400 --> 01:09:05,833
[Joel] Well, it's neat
and all but I'm tired.

1579
01:09:16,933 --> 01:09:18,067
[Tom] Wow.

1580
01:09:18,967 --> 01:09:22,000
This Bart Simpson stuff
is everywhere.

1581
01:09:22,000 --> 01:09:23,733
Let's not spend
all our money here.

1582
01:09:23,733 --> 01:09:25,733
We're only
on the outskirts of town.

1583
01:09:26,300 --> 01:09:28,067
[Crow] Yeah, there's
a gas station near my house

1584
01:09:28,067 --> 01:09:29,633
-that sells this junk.
-[clank]

1585
01:09:29,633 --> 01:09:31,567
[Joel] Sounds like your head,
Texan.

1586
01:09:33,467 --> 01:09:36,700
[Crow] Got salt shakers this big
in Texas, country boy?

1587
01:09:37,433 --> 01:09:39,067
[Dr. Eckstrom]
At least the equal of Earth.

1588
01:09:39,367 --> 01:09:40,933
[Joel] Or buy it from Pier 1.

1589
01:09:41,767 --> 01:09:44,567
[Van Horn] Do you think that
a complex organized society

1590
01:09:44,567 --> 01:09:46,167
existed here once?

1591
01:09:46,167 --> 01:09:48,300
[Dr. Eckstrom]
Yes, and from all indications,

1592
01:09:48,300 --> 01:09:49,733
thousands of years ago.

1593
01:09:50,267 --> 01:09:51,733
I wonder how it happened.

1594
01:09:52,200 --> 01:09:54,800
[Tom] Billions and billions
of years ago.

1595
01:09:55,367 --> 01:09:57,100
[Harry] But then it would have
created a depression

1596
01:09:57,100 --> 01:09:58,233
like a moon crater.

1597
01:09:58,233 --> 01:10:00,733
[Dr. Eckstrom] No.
This wasn't caused by a meteor.

1598
01:10:00,733 --> 01:10:02,633
[Crow]
This was no boating accident.

1599
01:10:02,633 --> 01:10:05,000
...blast effect
coupled with intense heat.

1600
01:10:05,000 --> 01:10:06,733
[Joel] I think it
was street gangs.

1601
01:10:06,733 --> 01:10:08,600
[Tom] I think it was
a drive-by shooting.

1602
01:10:08,600 --> 01:10:09,867
-[Tom] Yeah.
-[Joel] Yeah.

1603
01:10:11,067 --> 01:10:12,400
[Crow] Hey, look,

1604
01:10:12,767 --> 01:10:14,467
a big cow cream pitcher.

1605
01:10:19,300 --> 01:10:20,633
[Joel] My grandfather
went to Mars

1606
01:10:20,633 --> 01:10:22,633
and all I got was this
crummy T-shirt.

1607
01:10:25,667 --> 01:10:27,633
[Tom] That looks like
an Evinrude to me.

1608
01:10:27,633 --> 01:10:29,267
[Crow] Oh, look it's a Theremin.

1609
01:10:36,500 --> 01:10:37,933
You'd better stop.

1610
01:10:37,933 --> 01:10:40,267
Radioactivity
is at the danger level.

1611
01:10:40,267 --> 01:10:42,333
[Joel] And that's a bad thing.

1612
01:10:42,800 --> 01:10:43,900
[Crow] Right.

1613
01:10:47,800 --> 01:10:49,367
[Dr. Eckstrom] Ironic, isn't it?

1614
01:10:50,267 --> 01:10:51,767
The mind of man

1615
01:10:52,167 --> 01:10:53,700
wherever you encounter it,

1616
01:10:53,700 --> 01:10:55,500
Earth or Mars.

1617
01:10:55,500 --> 01:10:56,633
[Crow snoring]

1618
01:10:56,633 --> 01:10:58,800
The highest attainment
of human intellect...

1619
01:10:59,367 --> 01:11:00,933
[Crow coughing]

1620
01:11:03,267 --> 01:11:04,400
[Joel] Sorry.

1621
01:11:04,400 --> 01:11:05,567
[Crow] You can't swing
a dead cat

1622
01:11:05,567 --> 01:11:08,100
without hitting
one of his boring speeches.

1623
01:11:08,533 --> 01:11:09,833
...ruined like this.

1624
01:11:11,167 --> 01:11:13,367
[Floyd] Don't you think we ought
start back for the ship?

1625
01:11:13,367 --> 01:11:14,533
[William] I agree to that.

1626
01:11:14,900 --> 01:11:17,767
We're neither prepared nor
equipped for any stay here.

1627
01:11:17,767 --> 01:11:20,200
[Dr. Eckstrom] We have supplies
for several days.

1628
01:11:20,200 --> 01:11:23,467
Let's use this precious time
as effectively as we can.

1629
01:11:23,467 --> 01:11:24,700
[Crow] Let's all take a nap.

1630
01:11:24,700 --> 01:11:27,233
[Van Horn] Dr. Eckstrom,
a day here is more valuable

1631
01:11:27,233 --> 01:11:29,333
than years of research on Earth.

1632
01:11:30,067 --> 01:11:32,233
[Floyd] Well, okay
then let's get going.

1633
01:11:32,800 --> 01:11:34,000
[Tom] Okay, people.

1634
01:11:34,000 --> 01:11:36,300
Half an hour and we'll meet
at the Hot Sands, all right?

1635
01:11:40,500 --> 01:11:43,933
[Joel] And there
on the handle was a hook.

1636
01:11:44,500 --> 01:11:47,200
[Dr. Eckstrom] What a lesson
here for our world.

1637
01:11:47,467 --> 01:11:48,667
One blast...

1638
01:11:48,667 --> 01:11:50,700
[Joel] Now, what did we
learn today, Jim?

1639
01:11:50,700 --> 01:11:53,100
-[Crow] Not to steal.
-[Tom] That was yesterday.

1640
01:11:53,800 --> 01:11:55,833
[Van Horn] Could there
have been any survivors?

1641
01:11:56,467 --> 01:11:57,933
[Dr. Eckstrom] I should hate
to think so.

1642
01:11:59,800 --> 01:12:01,567
-[Crow] What-- Line please.
-[Joel] Line.

1643
01:12:01,833 --> 01:12:02,933
[Tom] Line.

1644
01:12:03,967 --> 01:12:07,067
We still don't know all the
genetic effects of radiation,

1645
01:12:07,067 --> 01:12:09,200
but that it will
produce mutation,

1646
01:12:09,200 --> 01:12:11,433
malformaties
disfigurements, blindness.

1647
01:12:11,433 --> 01:12:13,467
That much we're sure of
from research.

1648
01:12:14,933 --> 01:12:16,433
A blast like this...

1649
01:12:17,200 --> 01:12:19,833
I should hate to think
that any survived.

1650
01:12:20,367 --> 01:12:22,133
[Joel] Now goodnight, everybody.

1651
01:12:23,933 --> 01:12:25,867
[Harry] Why don't you
try to get some rest.

1652
01:12:27,133 --> 01:12:28,900
[William] If I ever
get back to Texas,

1653
01:12:28,900 --> 01:12:30,733
I'll know better
than to leave again.

1654
01:12:30,733 --> 01:12:32,767
[Crow] Oh, shut up.
Ever since we left,

1655
01:12:32,767 --> 01:12:35,233
all that's been out of you
is Texas this and Texas that.

1656
01:12:35,233 --> 01:12:36,300
Well, listen, bub.

1657
01:12:36,300 --> 01:12:38,833
I've been to Texas
and it's not all that great!

1658
01:12:39,333 --> 01:12:41,200
Sheesh! I'm leaving.

1659
01:12:41,733 --> 01:12:44,100
[Joel] We better
get out of here. I'll carry you.

1660
01:12:44,433 --> 01:12:45,933
[Tom] It was a dark...

1661
01:12:58,867 --> 01:13:00,767
What do you guys
want to do with your life?

1662
01:13:00,767 --> 01:13:02,433
What are your dreams?

1663
01:13:02,433 --> 01:13:05,433
I mean, where do you
want to be in two years?

1664
01:13:06,200 --> 01:13:08,367
Beauty walks the razor's edge.

1665
01:13:08,367 --> 01:13:09,867
Someday I'll make it mine.

1666
01:13:11,633 --> 01:13:14,200
[Tom] Bless the beasts
and the children.

1667
01:13:15,700 --> 01:13:18,067
Why do birds suddenly appear

1668
01:13:18,633 --> 01:13:20,933
every time you walk near?

1669
01:13:22,800 --> 01:13:27,200
All I want is a tall ship
and a star to sail her by.

1670
01:13:29,333 --> 01:13:32,433
[Tom] Oz never did give
nothing to the Tin Man.

1671
01:13:34,367 --> 01:13:37,300
One is the loneliest number

1672
01:13:38,100 --> 01:13:39,833
that you'll ever do.

1673
01:13:41,700 --> 01:13:44,067
I dreamed I saw
Joe Hill last night.

1674
01:13:46,833 --> 01:13:48,033
[Tom] Half-breed.

1675
01:13:48,233 --> 01:13:50,100
That's all I ever heard.

1676
01:13:51,567 --> 01:13:55,900
We had joy, we had fun,
we had seasons in the sun.

1677
01:13:58,867 --> 01:14:03,667
But the stars that we reached
were just starfish on the beach.

1678
01:14:07,500 --> 01:14:10,133
[Tom] Dames like this
always got beer around.

1679
01:14:10,133 --> 01:14:11,200
What?

1680
01:14:11,200 --> 01:14:12,533
[Tom] Oh, I mean...

1681
01:14:13,033 --> 01:14:15,100
filled with mangled cream
and amber

1682
01:14:15,100 --> 01:14:19,433
I will drain that classic aim
such hilarious visions clamber.

1683
01:14:21,067 --> 01:14:22,733
-Oh.
-[Joel] Oh.

1684
01:14:22,733 --> 01:14:23,867
[Crow] Yeah.

1685
01:14:25,267 --> 01:14:27,100
Hey, there's a ship out there.

1686
01:14:28,100 --> 01:14:29,367
Who said that?

1687
01:14:29,367 --> 01:14:30,567
I did.

1688
01:14:30,567 --> 01:14:31,833
[Tom] Oh, that's beautiful.

1689
01:14:31,833 --> 01:14:35,333
[Crow] No, no, no. There's...
really, there's a ship outside.

1690
01:14:35,567 --> 01:14:37,100
It just goes on like that?

1691
01:14:37,100 --> 01:14:39,767
No.
Outside, there's a ship outside.

1692
01:14:39,767 --> 01:14:40,900
[Tom] Oh, you mean

1693
01:14:40,900 --> 01:14:42,700
mean outside the bounds
of earthly restraints.

1694
01:14:42,700 --> 01:14:45,033
No, no! Out there!
See look!

1695
01:14:45,033 --> 01:14:47,067
-[Joel] Yikes. He's right.
-[Tom] There's a ship out there.

1696
01:14:47,067 --> 01:14:49,233
Hey, Cam-bot,
give me a rocket number 9.

1697
01:14:49,233 --> 01:14:52,000
I need an exterior to the ship.
Let's go, you guys.

1698
01:14:54,533 --> 01:14:56,167
[Joel] There's somebody
outside the ship,

1699
01:14:56,167 --> 01:14:58,767
-and they're trying to hail us.
-[Tom] Huh?

1700
01:14:58,767 --> 01:15:01,067
Check it out on
the Hexfield Viewscreen.

1701
01:15:03,833 --> 01:15:05,867
Hello, puny Earthling.

1702
01:15:06,967 --> 01:15:09,967
[Tom] Oh hi. I think
she's talking to Joel.

1703
01:15:09,967 --> 01:15:13,533
I've come these many light years
to find you and now,

1704
01:15:13,533 --> 01:15:16,333
you and your daughter
are doomed.

1705
01:15:16,767 --> 01:15:19,267
[Crow] Maybe she's
talking about Gypsy, Joel.

1706
01:15:19,267 --> 01:15:21,600
You and your daughter
will never escape

1707
01:15:21,600 --> 01:15:23,567
the power of the dark one.

1708
01:15:23,867 --> 01:15:25,367
[Joel] Dark one?

1709
01:15:25,367 --> 01:15:26,700
Doug Warren?

1710
01:15:26,700 --> 01:15:28,800
I haven't seen him
since high school.

1711
01:15:28,800 --> 01:15:31,133
Dark one.
I don't know any dark one.

1712
01:15:31,133 --> 01:15:32,567
You dare to mock me?

1713
01:15:32,800 --> 01:15:35,733
See if you can mock me
in the room of questions.

1714
01:15:35,733 --> 01:15:38,500
Uh, Joel, who is she
and what the heck does she mean?

1715
01:15:38,500 --> 01:15:39,767
Just let me handle this.

1716
01:15:39,767 --> 01:15:42,533
I know how to talk
to these space vixens.

1717
01:15:44,300 --> 01:15:49,467
Uh, I could never
give into your... demands.

1718
01:15:49,867 --> 01:15:54,400
You'll never get
any satisfaction out of me.

1719
01:15:55,067 --> 01:15:56,333
Not even a little?

1720
01:15:56,900 --> 01:15:58,000
No, I...

1721
01:15:58,367 --> 01:15:59,933
God she's ugly.

1722
01:15:59,933 --> 01:16:02,433
Okay, well then got to go.
Bye-bye.

1723
01:16:02,433 --> 01:16:03,867
-Wait, wait.
-[speaks alien language]

1724
01:16:03,867 --> 01:16:05,267
Wait, wait, wait. Joel.

1725
01:16:05,267 --> 01:16:07,600
Maybe she can help us
get back down to Earth.

1726
01:16:07,600 --> 01:16:10,833
Yeah.
Can you rescue us or help us?

1727
01:16:11,433 --> 01:16:13,967
To Earth? I don't know.
What does it look like?

1728
01:16:14,433 --> 01:16:16,567
We have to get down to Earth.

1729
01:16:17,233 --> 01:16:20,233
How nice for you.
Got to go. Ciao. Bye-bye.

1730
01:16:20,233 --> 01:16:21,533
[speaks alien language]

1731
01:16:21,533 --> 01:16:23,533
-I live to serve you.
-Bye-bye.

1732
01:16:23,533 --> 01:16:25,700
-[Crow] Whoa, whoa.
-[Tom] Hey!

1733
01:16:26,833 --> 01:16:28,333
Oh, man.

1734
01:16:28,333 --> 01:16:30,767
[Tom] What a strange woman.

1735
01:16:30,767 --> 01:16:32,700
Kind of makes you think,
though, doesn't it?

1736
01:16:32,700 --> 01:16:33,800
[Joel, Crow] Yeah.

1737
01:16:33,800 --> 01:16:35,500
Guess you don't have to be
a rocket scientist

1738
01:16:35,500 --> 01:16:38,333
to pilot a rocketship.

1739
01:16:38,333 --> 01:16:41,400
Well, at least we've still
got each other, you guys.

1740
01:16:41,400 --> 01:16:44,500
Yeah. No matter where
I serve my guests,

1741
01:16:44,500 --> 01:16:47,133
it seems they like
the kitchen best.

1742
01:16:47,133 --> 01:16:50,167
Yeah. And if you don't believe
the dead come back to life,

1743
01:16:50,167 --> 01:16:52,233
you should see this place
around quitting time.

1744
01:16:52,233 --> 01:16:54,100
-[Crow laughs]
-Yeah.

1745
01:16:54,100 --> 01:16:57,267
It's a nice day
for a white wedding.

1746
01:16:57,267 --> 01:16:58,800
We got commercial sign.

1747
01:16:58,800 --> 01:17:01,067
Hey, I'm moved.
Who said that?

1748
01:17:01,067 --> 01:17:02,367
[Tom] Was that Blake?

1749
01:17:02,367 --> 01:17:04,467
Yeats perhaps, or Rod McKuen?

1750
01:17:04,467 --> 01:17:05,800
Hmm.

1751
01:17:06,033 --> 01:17:10,267
[music playing]

1752
01:17:13,800 --> 01:17:16,733
[Joel] You don't have to be
crazy to work here, but help.

1753
01:17:17,633 --> 01:17:18,800
[Crow chuckles]

1754
01:17:19,267 --> 01:17:22,167
[Tom] Meanwhile,
here in the day for night scene.

1755
01:17:22,767 --> 01:17:24,533
It'd be a good place
for a water slide over there,

1756
01:17:24,533 --> 01:17:25,700
don't you think?

1757
01:17:33,400 --> 01:17:36,300
[Crow] I'm going to go out
and discover a new race

1758
01:17:36,300 --> 01:17:37,767
and show 'em all.

1759
01:17:38,167 --> 01:17:40,967
This is my side of the mountain.
I'll show those guys.

1760
01:17:40,967 --> 01:17:42,967
I'll live in a tree.
Yeah, that's great.

1761
01:17:42,967 --> 01:17:44,100
Make boomerangs.

1762
01:17:44,100 --> 01:17:45,800
[Joel] Snuggle close, friend.

1763
01:17:47,633 --> 01:17:48,900
[Crow] I'll show 'em.

1764
01:17:55,167 --> 01:17:56,633
[Joel] I'm kind of tired.

1765
01:17:58,633 --> 01:17:59,867
[Tom] Oh, man.

1766
01:17:59,867 --> 01:18:03,267
Either I'm high or there's about
50 Martian dudes down there.

1767
01:18:03,267 --> 01:18:05,233
Oh, man, game over.

1768
01:18:05,233 --> 01:18:07,000
Oh, I got to lay off the butane.

1769
01:18:07,000 --> 01:18:09,167
[Joel] Hey, they're
shooting 2001 down there.

1770
01:18:09,900 --> 01:18:11,100
[William] Bill, Floyd,
Dr. Eckstrom.

1771
01:18:11,100 --> 01:18:13,033
[Crow] Reese... Youngblood.

1772
01:18:16,033 --> 01:18:18,467
[Tom] Maybe I'm crazy,
but I just saw our ancestors

1773
01:18:18,467 --> 01:18:20,267
and they're walking erect.

1774
01:18:20,867 --> 01:18:23,000
[Crow] You squirting glue
in that mask?

1775
01:18:23,700 --> 01:18:25,633
It wasn't a nightmare.
I haven't slept at all.

1776
01:18:25,967 --> 01:18:27,833
-I tell you I saw them!
-Where?

1777
01:18:27,833 --> 01:18:28,867
Right out there.

1778
01:18:29,533 --> 01:18:31,100
And when I yelled,
they disappeared.

1779
01:18:31,867 --> 01:18:33,200
All right. Let's go see.

1780
01:18:33,200 --> 01:18:35,433
[Joel] It was the cast of
Chorus Line, I swear.

1781
01:18:36,400 --> 01:18:38,300
[Crow] Let's all run like fools
toward the danger!

1782
01:18:38,300 --> 01:18:39,933
[Tom] Serpentine everybody!

1783
01:18:39,933 --> 01:18:42,100
Incoming wounded!

1784
01:18:45,067 --> 01:18:46,567
[Joel]
I know they're around here.

1785
01:18:46,867 --> 01:18:47,867
Right here.

1786
01:18:48,100 --> 01:18:50,233
When they heard me,
they disappeared in there.

1787
01:18:52,067 --> 01:18:55,533
[Joel] Yeah, and you were there
and you, and you and everybody.

1788
01:18:56,567 --> 01:18:58,267
[William]
It don't take a Navajo

1789
01:18:58,267 --> 01:19:00,067
to figure out what caused these.

1790
01:19:00,067 --> 01:19:01,600
[Crow] Yeah,
and we're rocket scientists.

1791
01:19:01,600 --> 01:19:03,667
[William] Now maybe you'll
agree to return to the ship.

1792
01:19:04,267 --> 01:19:06,833
[Joel] Oh, come on sissy pants.

1793
01:19:06,833 --> 01:19:07,900
...kind of creatures they are.

1794
01:19:07,900 --> 01:19:09,767
[Tom]
Well, they definitely have feet.

1795
01:19:10,067 --> 01:19:11,467
[Harry] We don't know
how many there are?

1796
01:19:11,467 --> 01:19:12,733
And suppose they're hostile,

1797
01:19:12,733 --> 01:19:15,033
with one rifle and a pistol.
We won't have a prayer.

1798
01:19:15,033 --> 01:19:16,900
[Dr. Eckstrom] That's the chance
we have to take.

1799
01:19:16,900 --> 01:19:18,567
[Joel] And you're taking it.

1800
01:19:21,167 --> 01:19:23,267
[Dr. Eckstrom] You wait here.
I'm going ahead.

1801
01:19:23,267 --> 01:19:25,433
[William] This kind of thing is
just my meat.

1802
01:19:25,433 --> 01:19:26,767
Mind if I come along?

1803
01:19:27,467 --> 01:19:29,700
-[Dr. Eckstrom] Thank you.
-I'm with you, Doctor.

1804
01:19:29,700 --> 01:19:31,600
[Dr. Eckstrom] You three stay.

1805
01:19:31,600 --> 01:19:33,033
I'll have to make that an order.

1806
01:19:33,433 --> 01:19:34,533
Come on, Bill.

1807
01:19:34,867 --> 01:19:37,567
[Crow] Yeah, let's go kill
something we don't understand.

1808
01:19:37,567 --> 01:19:38,833
[Tom] I'm bringing my pool cue

1809
01:19:38,833 --> 01:19:40,833
in case they start
ranking on our women.

1810
01:19:42,833 --> 01:19:44,567
[Joel] Does anybody
got any lines?

1811
01:19:45,967 --> 01:19:49,867
[Crow] Well, we'll just wait
here then, shall we?

1812
01:19:50,700 --> 01:19:51,733
Right.

1813
01:19:52,700 --> 01:19:54,233
[Joel] We could play Pictionary.

1814
01:19:54,233 --> 01:19:57,300
Oh, I know,
I spy with my little eye.

1815
01:20:00,767 --> 01:20:01,800
Wait.

1816
01:20:03,433 --> 01:20:05,267
They're there.
There they go. Wait.

1817
01:20:05,267 --> 01:20:07,433
No, no, don't...
don't shoot too high.

1818
01:20:07,433 --> 01:20:08,767
Lead them on.

1819
01:20:12,333 --> 01:20:14,800
[all] Help! I've fallen
and I can't get up.

1820
01:20:14,800 --> 01:20:16,833
[Tom] I'm having chest pains.

1821
01:20:16,833 --> 01:20:20,967
[Joel, Tom, Crow]
Boom chaka laka...

1822
01:20:25,533 --> 01:20:27,733
[Joel] You know,
she's not half bad.

1823
01:20:27,733 --> 01:20:29,733
Wonder if she'd
like to evolve up.

1824
01:20:30,267 --> 01:20:31,900
She's moving up
in the food chain in my eyes,

1825
01:20:31,900 --> 01:20:33,100
if you know what I mean.

1826
01:20:33,100 --> 01:20:35,067
[Crow] Hello, Federal.

1827
01:20:41,200 --> 01:20:43,367
[Joel] Now we've
never met before, have we?

1828
01:20:44,033 --> 01:20:45,800
[screaming]

1829
01:20:45,800 --> 01:20:47,100
[Tom] No, it's okay.

1830
01:20:47,100 --> 01:20:49,233
He won't tell another story.
I promise.

1831
01:20:50,667 --> 01:20:52,333
[Joel] Oh my goodness.

1832
01:20:55,567 --> 01:20:58,133
[Joel] Geez, it's an
entire race of mimes.

1833
01:20:58,133 --> 01:21:00,333
We got to get back
and warn Earth.

1834
01:21:03,733 --> 01:21:05,867
[Crow] Oh, yuck,
unfortunate skin.

1835
01:21:05,867 --> 01:21:08,867
[Joel] Boom chaka laka...

1836
01:21:13,600 --> 01:21:15,267
Atomic Age...

1837
01:21:15,633 --> 01:21:16,833
to Stone Age.

1838
01:21:16,833 --> 01:21:19,233
[Tom] In less than
30 minutes guaranteed.

1839
01:21:20,167 --> 01:21:23,333
[Tom] Message coming in, sir!

1840
01:21:27,233 --> 01:21:28,267
Bill!

1841
01:21:30,567 --> 01:21:33,267
[Tom] They got rocks that big
in Texas, Bill?

1842
01:21:40,600 --> 01:21:43,533
[Crow] Thank you,
but I already have one of those.

1843
01:21:43,533 --> 01:21:46,833
[Joel] Sorry to have interrupted
your production of the lottery.

1844
01:21:47,367 --> 01:21:49,900
[Tom] Thanks for the rocks.
Here's some bullets for you.

1845
01:21:57,467 --> 01:21:58,667
[gunshots]

1846
01:21:58,667 --> 01:21:59,900
[Crow] Here's some more.

1847
01:21:59,900 --> 01:22:02,467
[Joel] I guess I embarrassed
them in front of their chicks.

1848
01:22:03,567 --> 01:22:05,333
[Joel, Tom] Boom chaka laka...

1849
01:22:05,333 --> 01:22:07,200
[Crow mumbles something]

1850
01:22:09,800 --> 01:22:13,800
[Tom] ♪ Valderi, Valdera,
Valderi, Valdera ♪

1851
01:22:21,600 --> 01:22:23,567
[Crow] Lead him on.
Don't throw too hard.

1852
01:22:29,033 --> 01:22:32,133
Well. Hey, Steve,
how'd everything go?

1853
01:22:41,933 --> 01:22:43,500
[Van Horn] Dr. Eckstrom.

1854
01:22:46,500 --> 01:22:50,600
[Joel] Big... mean... guy...
throw rock down... over there.

1855
01:22:51,067 --> 01:22:52,200
[Floyd] Where's Bill?

1856
01:22:52,667 --> 01:22:55,600
[Joel] I brought back
a souvenir.

1857
01:22:56,267 --> 01:22:57,700
[Crow] Sounds like...

1858
01:22:57,700 --> 01:23:02,600
um... sounds like fall, fall,
tall, tall down, tall down.

1859
01:23:03,467 --> 01:23:05,567
[Joel] You move his head.
I'll do the voice.

1860
01:23:05,567 --> 01:23:07,133
[Floyd] Murdering savages.

1861
01:23:08,133 --> 01:23:09,233
[Dr. Eckstrom] No, Floyd.

1862
01:23:10,267 --> 01:23:12,467
They are crazed...

1863
01:23:12,467 --> 01:23:15,700
[Joel] Brother, he's dying
and he's still giving speeches.

1864
01:23:16,267 --> 01:23:18,567
-[Dr. Eckstrom mumbles]
-[Van Horn] Don't talk anymore.

1865
01:23:18,567 --> 01:23:20,700
[Dr. Eckstrom] You must
get back to Earth.

1866
01:23:20,933 --> 01:23:22,500
Tell them what we found.

1867
01:23:23,333 --> 01:23:24,633
Maybe this will...

1868
01:23:24,900 --> 01:23:26,400
[Crow] Will... Will what?

1869
01:23:27,067 --> 01:23:29,667
Fall down?
Play dead? What?

1870
01:23:31,800 --> 01:23:34,700
[Joel] Now what do we do?
He's got the keys.

1871
01:23:36,533 --> 01:23:38,233
Thanks. We've got plenty.

1872
01:23:40,133 --> 01:23:44,800
[Joel, Tom, Crow]
Boom chaka laka...

1873
01:23:47,467 --> 01:23:51,133
[Joel, Tom, Crow] ♪ Valderi,
Valdera, Valderi, Valdera ♪

1874
01:23:53,633 --> 01:23:57,200
[Joel, Tom, Crow]
Boom chaka laka...

1875
01:23:58,333 --> 01:24:00,567
[Tom] ♪ Valderi, Valdera,
Valderi, Valdera ♪

1876
01:24:06,767 --> 01:24:09,500
[Joel] Well, at least he
hasn't been hit by a rock yet.

1877
01:24:09,933 --> 01:24:12,600
[Tom] Oh. Good shot.

1878
01:24:12,600 --> 01:24:14,633
[Joel] That's just my spine,
sir.

1879
01:24:15,067 --> 01:24:17,267
[Crow] Come back
and you'll get more of the same.

1880
01:24:17,833 --> 01:24:19,767
[Joel] Val... deri...

1881
01:24:20,500 --> 01:24:22,033
Val... dera...

1882
01:24:23,267 --> 01:24:24,467
[Crow] Take that.

1883
01:24:24,867 --> 01:24:26,900
[Joel] Well, that
could have gone better.

1884
01:24:26,900 --> 01:24:29,233
-[Tom] Help, Mr. Wizard.
-[Crow] Nonsense.

1885
01:24:29,233 --> 01:24:31,300
[Joel] Run away.
Run away.

1886
01:24:31,300 --> 01:24:33,700
[Tom] I don't want to be
an astronaut anymore.

1887
01:24:33,700 --> 01:24:37,267
[Joel] Oh, Scooby, we got to
get out of here, Scooby.

1888
01:24:37,267 --> 01:24:39,067
[Crow German accent] Drizzle,
drazzle, drazzle, drome,

1889
01:24:39,067 --> 01:24:41,567
Time for this one to come home.
Ho ho.

1890
01:24:42,733 --> 01:24:46,800
[theme music playing]

1891
01:24:53,567 --> 01:24:55,700
[Joel] Where do
you want to be in two years?

1892
01:24:59,833 --> 01:25:01,767
I wish Harry were able to help.

1893
01:25:01,767 --> 01:25:04,467
It's impossible to get
a precise result without radar.

1894
01:25:04,467 --> 01:25:06,400
You have done a wonderful job.

1895
01:25:07,633 --> 01:25:09,900
We should have
fuel enough for landing.

1896
01:25:09,900 --> 01:25:11,700
When we feel
the Earth's gravity?

1897
01:25:11,700 --> 01:25:13,833
As soon as there's
an increase in speed.

1898
01:25:13,833 --> 01:25:15,800
According to elapsed time
and velocity chart,

1899
01:25:15,800 --> 01:25:17,233
almost any minute now.

1900
01:25:17,233 --> 01:25:18,900
But without a check
from Harry, it's...

1901
01:25:18,900 --> 01:25:20,267
I know we're in a tight spot.

1902
01:25:20,267 --> 01:25:22,933
[Crow] Where are we going
to cash a check out here?

1903
01:25:23,433 --> 01:25:24,867
[Joel]
Lloyd, you're flying a rocket,

1904
01:25:24,867 --> 01:25:26,667
not balancing
the household budget.

1905
01:25:26,667 --> 01:25:27,933
Get with it.

1906
01:25:32,500 --> 01:25:34,967
[Tom] Okay, now, uh,
where did he put the charts?

1907
01:25:34,967 --> 01:25:36,267
Go ugh!

1908
01:25:36,533 --> 01:25:37,933
There. All better.

1909
01:25:42,300 --> 01:25:43,933
Any sign of increase in speed?

1910
01:25:43,933 --> 01:25:44,933
Not yet.

1911
01:25:45,300 --> 01:25:46,833
[Crow] How's the plot doing?

1912
01:25:47,433 --> 01:25:50,233
You know, you're a pretty
swell girl, Lisa.

1913
01:25:51,133 --> 01:25:53,633
[Joel] Oh, now that everyone's
dead I'm a pretty swell girl.

1914
01:25:53,633 --> 01:25:55,067
I'm not Dr. Van Horn anymore?

1915
01:25:55,600 --> 01:25:57,533
No. Just Lisa.

1916
01:25:58,967 --> 01:26:00,100
Doing her job.

1917
01:26:01,300 --> 01:26:03,167
No. I don't
see it that way at all.

1918
01:26:03,867 --> 01:26:07,100
I see a woman sweet, gentle.

1919
01:26:08,433 --> 01:26:09,700
[Crow] And when I
get back to Earth,

1920
01:26:09,700 --> 01:26:11,800
I'm going to call this girl,
but in the meantime,

1921
01:26:11,800 --> 01:26:13,267
love the one you're with.

1922
01:26:13,267 --> 01:26:14,600
Somehow, no.

1923
01:26:14,600 --> 01:26:17,567
But I am, you know,
the same I mean.

1924
01:26:17,967 --> 01:26:19,967
Well, maybe I've
changed then but...

1925
01:26:20,433 --> 01:26:23,333
I feel I've... I've never
known you before.

1926
01:26:24,600 --> 01:26:28,033
Will you... do something for me,
please?

1927
01:26:28,033 --> 01:26:29,267
Sure. What?

1928
01:26:30,767 --> 01:26:33,000
Say my name the way
you did a moment ago.

1929
01:26:33,200 --> 01:26:34,433
[Crow] Cookie pants?

1930
01:26:35,200 --> 01:26:36,267
Lisa.

1931
01:26:37,567 --> 01:26:39,133
[Tom] Oh, Lisa.

1932
01:26:39,133 --> 01:26:41,733
[Crow] Hey, Mr. Douglas, I see
you're doing some shooshin'.

1933
01:26:42,267 --> 01:26:43,900
RXM calling BWS.

1934
01:26:43,900 --> 01:26:45,133
[Joel] What's the matter, boy?

1935
01:26:45,467 --> 01:26:46,933
Dream about rabbits?

1936
01:26:46,933 --> 01:26:48,500
Fire in Dead Rock Canyon?

1937
01:26:48,500 --> 01:26:49,767
Come in, DWS.

1938
01:26:49,767 --> 01:26:52,633
[Crow] Oh, ick. She's swabbing
him with a jock strap.

1939
01:26:53,967 --> 01:26:55,167
They won't answer.

1940
01:26:55,800 --> 01:26:58,033
-They will never answer.
-Of course they will.

1941
01:26:58,033 --> 01:26:59,833
Speed's increasing.
We're going to make it.

1942
01:26:59,833 --> 01:27:00,967
Shut off the fuel.

1943
01:27:00,967 --> 01:27:02,067
[Joel] All right.

1944
01:27:02,067 --> 01:27:03,167
[Tom] Oh, yeah.

1945
01:27:06,000 --> 01:27:08,767
[Joel] Uh, about that fuel,
Lloyd.

1946
01:27:15,600 --> 01:27:17,933
[Tom] Well, the good news is
that we won't make a big

1947
01:27:17,933 --> 01:27:20,200
explosion when we hit the Earth.
[chuckles]

1948
01:27:20,200 --> 01:27:21,200
[Crow] Heh.

1949
01:27:27,933 --> 01:27:31,933
[Crow] Kimmie, honey,
where'd you put the extra fuel?

1950
01:27:33,567 --> 01:27:34,700
I'm sorry.

1951
01:27:36,433 --> 01:27:38,733
-I wish I could help.
-[Tom] Tuck him in, Joel.

1952
01:27:38,733 --> 01:27:39,900
You heard what Floyd said.

1953
01:27:40,600 --> 01:27:42,000
Everything will be all right.

1954
01:27:43,200 --> 01:27:44,600
About the fuel...

1955
01:27:45,067 --> 01:27:46,233
we lost so much.

1956
01:27:46,867 --> 01:27:48,700
We'll have enough
for a landing.

1957
01:27:52,333 --> 01:27:54,067
[Tom] He was thirsty,
he drank it.

1958
01:27:55,667 --> 01:27:59,067
[Crow] Well, this should really
test Lloyd's sunny disposition.

1959
01:28:01,900 --> 01:28:03,200
How are we doing?

1960
01:28:05,067 --> 01:28:07,167
-Not so good.
-Why? What's the matter?

1961
01:28:07,167 --> 01:28:09,467
Everything's worked out
so far right in the nose.

1962
01:28:10,233 --> 01:28:12,200
We haven't got
enough fuel for a landing.

1963
01:28:12,200 --> 01:28:13,367
[Tom] Have enough
fuel for a crash?

1964
01:28:13,367 --> 01:28:15,000
Not even for the approach.

1965
01:28:15,000 --> 01:28:16,400
But the motors
were functioning perfectly

1966
01:28:16,400 --> 01:28:17,700
before I shut them off.

1967
01:28:18,400 --> 01:28:20,233
At one tenth capacity.

1968
01:28:20,767 --> 01:28:23,100
[crying] I'm sorry.
It's all my fault.

1969
01:28:23,100 --> 01:28:24,600
Maybe it's only the instruments.

1970
01:28:25,467 --> 01:28:27,867
[Tom] Just keep telling
yourself that, Lloyd.

1971
01:28:34,767 --> 01:28:36,100
It's hopeless.

1972
01:28:36,100 --> 01:28:38,200
[Joel] So much for the
power of positive thinking,

1973
01:28:38,200 --> 01:28:39,433
huh, Lloyd?

1974
01:28:40,200 --> 01:28:42,733
[Crow] Could they steer it
into a willow tree or something?

1975
01:28:44,333 --> 01:28:46,800
All of Dr. Eckstrom's work
in vain.

1976
01:28:46,800 --> 01:28:49,133
[Tom] His work?
Uh, what about your life?

1977
01:28:49,133 --> 01:28:52,333
Now the world will never know
the terrible truth we learned.

1978
01:28:53,700 --> 01:28:54,700
No.

1979
01:28:55,400 --> 01:28:57,067
Maybe there's still a chance.

1980
01:28:58,933 --> 01:29:00,767
We must be close enough
for short wave.

1981
01:29:01,967 --> 01:29:03,600
Try to contact the base.

1982
01:29:04,100 --> 01:29:05,867
If we only could.

1983
01:29:10,933 --> 01:29:12,600
We must report everything.

1984
01:29:13,033 --> 01:29:15,500
Tell them as much as we can,

1985
01:29:16,567 --> 01:29:17,733
what we saw,

1986
01:29:18,700 --> 01:29:20,400
the mistakes that we made.

1987
01:29:21,500 --> 01:29:24,800
[Crow] Okay. Story so far -
we got lost, flew to Phoenix,

1988
01:29:24,800 --> 01:29:26,567
got attacked,
we're going to die.

1989
01:29:26,567 --> 01:29:28,367
[Joel] Doomed spaceship
to Earth.

1990
01:29:28,367 --> 01:29:30,367
Doomed spaceship to Earth.

1991
01:29:31,100 --> 01:29:32,600
-Dr. Fleming?
-Yes?

1992
01:29:32,600 --> 01:29:34,300
The radio control room
just called.

1993
01:29:34,300 --> 01:29:35,833
They've contacted the RXM.

1994
01:29:36,233 --> 01:29:37,367
[Joel] There's a Mister...

1995
01:29:37,367 --> 01:29:40,067
Oh my God, my hair
is on fire on line one, sir.

1996
01:29:41,133 --> 01:29:43,833
You know, we really should have
gotten adjoining offices.

1997
01:29:43,833 --> 01:29:46,633
This is really quite a long walk
when you think about it.

1998
01:29:46,633 --> 01:29:47,733
[Tom] I think Rudy's.

1999
01:29:47,733 --> 01:29:49,633
[Joel]
Going to Rudy's? Yeah, okay.

2000
01:29:54,733 --> 01:29:57,033
There's too much interference.
We've got to use these, Doctor.

2001
01:29:59,100 --> 01:30:01,433
[Crow]
What? I can't see a thing.

2002
01:30:02,533 --> 01:30:04,367
[Tom] Is this Strauss?

2003
01:30:06,167 --> 01:30:08,467
BWS, Fleming speaking.

2004
01:30:09,100 --> 01:30:10,300
Calling RXM.

2005
01:30:11,000 --> 01:30:12,467
Come in, RXM.

2006
01:30:15,100 --> 01:30:17,367
[Crow] Help.

2007
01:30:21,533 --> 01:30:23,567
Repeat that again,
please, Dr. Van Horn.

2008
01:30:23,567 --> 01:30:26,667
-[Crow] I said...
-[Joel, Tom, Crow] Help!

2009
01:30:28,733 --> 01:30:34,567
-[Crow] H-E-L-P...
-[Tom] It's in Greek.

2010
01:30:34,567 --> 01:30:37,967
-[Crow] ...pronounced
-[all] Help!

2011
01:30:42,400 --> 01:30:43,933
What about Dr. Eckstrom?

2012
01:30:44,600 --> 01:30:46,667
[Joel] Uh, door nail,

2013
01:30:47,500 --> 01:30:49,800
daisies, worm food,

2014
01:30:50,533 --> 01:30:52,967
thrown in the sponge.
How many feet under?

2015
01:30:53,567 --> 01:30:54,667
Joined who?

2016
01:30:55,233 --> 01:30:56,300
[Tom] Ooh.

2017
01:31:00,133 --> 01:31:01,667
[Crow] Why the long face?

2018
01:31:01,667 --> 01:31:04,933
[Joel] But did he say
he loved me?

2019
01:31:08,300 --> 01:31:09,333
[Tom] Aw.

2020
01:31:12,567 --> 01:31:15,200
[Joel] My life's over, friends.
Come on. Spread out...

2021
01:31:15,200 --> 01:31:16,867
Oh, what the hell.

2022
01:31:21,433 --> 01:31:23,400
[Crow sniffling]

2023
01:31:23,400 --> 01:31:27,367
[Crow crying]

2024
01:31:34,633 --> 01:31:37,767
[Joel] I thought worm food
was a bit strong, Lloyd.

2025
01:31:47,067 --> 01:31:49,167
Where do you want to be
in two years?

2026
01:31:49,167 --> 01:31:51,167
Uh, I mean two minutes?

2027
01:31:52,767 --> 01:31:55,000
Floyd, hold me.

2028
01:31:55,000 --> 01:31:56,233
Hold me tight.

2029
01:31:56,233 --> 01:31:58,333
[Crow] If I held you any
tighter, I'd be in back of you.

2030
01:31:58,333 --> 01:31:59,433
[Tom imitates drum sound]

2031
01:31:59,433 --> 01:32:01,633
I'm sorry that
we haven't had any time,

2032
01:32:01,633 --> 01:32:04,033
that we didn't meet and know
each other the way we...

2033
01:32:04,033 --> 01:32:06,533
[Joel] Hey, we've got the
rest of our lives together.

2034
01:32:06,533 --> 01:32:08,767
-Oh, yes, yes.
-Don't.

2035
01:32:10,067 --> 01:32:12,200
We can say that time's
behind us.

2036
01:32:12,200 --> 01:32:14,333
-We've had years together.
-Yes.

2037
01:32:14,333 --> 01:32:16,833
I can tell you what a thousand
wonderful hours we spent

2038
01:32:16,833 --> 01:32:18,567
together because
I feel we would have.

2039
01:32:18,567 --> 01:32:20,133
[Tom]
It's all community service.

2040
01:32:20,433 --> 01:32:23,600
There's not that much difference
between the future and the past.

2041
01:32:24,133 --> 01:32:26,433
[Joel] You are high.

2042
01:32:30,967 --> 01:32:32,233
Shouldn't we wake him?

2043
01:32:32,233 --> 01:32:34,900
[Crow] Why? So he can experience
his own fiery death?

2044
01:32:34,900 --> 01:32:37,800
[Joel] Get your shoes on, honey.
We're about to die.

2045
01:32:37,800 --> 01:32:39,500
[Floyd] Try not to be afraid.

2046
01:32:41,600 --> 01:32:42,700
Floyd...

2047
01:32:44,300 --> 01:32:45,667
I'm not afraid anymore.

2048
01:32:45,667 --> 01:32:46,933
[Tom] Neither am I.

2049
01:32:47,600 --> 01:32:50,667
Something happened like a great
wave carrying us home,

2050
01:32:50,667 --> 01:32:53,067
bearing us, protecting us.

2051
01:32:53,500 --> 01:32:55,200
[Joel] Uh, where's...

2052
01:32:55,200 --> 01:32:56,833
[Tom] Woohoo.

2053
01:32:57,333 --> 01:32:59,267
[explosion]

2054
01:33:03,067 --> 01:33:05,167
[Joel] We scraped off
the runway, sir.

2055
01:33:05,433 --> 01:33:07,333
[man] The men from the news
syndicates are still waiting.

2056
01:33:07,800 --> 01:33:09,533
[Fleming]
All right. Let them come in.

2057
01:33:12,867 --> 01:33:14,067
[Joel] [bong]

2058
01:33:14,900 --> 01:33:16,333
[Tom] Keep it down, boys.

2059
01:33:17,500 --> 01:33:19,467
Dr. Fleming. The pilot and crew

2060
01:33:19,467 --> 01:33:21,367
of flight 19
International Airlines

2061
01:33:21,367 --> 01:33:24,367
observed a strange object
falling over Nova Scotia.

2062
01:33:24,567 --> 01:33:26,167
[Joel] Those were my friends.

2063
01:33:26,167 --> 01:33:27,833
[man with mustache]
It could have been the RXM.

2064
01:33:28,367 --> 01:33:30,367
My office has been
getting the same story

2065
01:33:30,367 --> 01:33:31,767
over the wires for hours.

2066
01:33:31,767 --> 01:33:33,933
We know the RXM
had been overdue a long time.

2067
01:33:33,933 --> 01:33:35,533
Is there any connection, doctor?

2068
01:33:38,767 --> 01:33:40,767
[Crow] Why doctor,
you've been crying.

2069
01:33:40,767 --> 01:33:43,333
As yet, there has been
no confirmation, but...

2070
01:33:44,100 --> 01:33:46,100
yes, I believe
there is a connection.

2071
01:33:46,300 --> 01:33:48,333
Then they all perished
in the crash.

2072
01:33:48,867 --> 01:33:50,533
No. Two were lost earlier.

2073
01:33:51,167 --> 01:33:53,067
But the flight must
be considered a failure.

2074
01:33:54,233 --> 01:33:55,467
[Joel] -Uh-oh.
-A failure?

2075
01:33:56,100 --> 01:33:58,700
-Every point of our...
-[Joel] Get out!

2076
01:33:59,300 --> 01:34:02,133
It has proven that interspace
travel is not only possible,

2077
01:34:02,133 --> 01:34:03,333
but practical.

2078
01:34:03,333 --> 01:34:05,900
[Crow] And enjoyable
for all members of the family.

2079
01:34:05,900 --> 01:34:07,300
Oh, there will be carnage.

2080
01:34:07,300 --> 01:34:10,100
[Fleming] ...which may well mean
the salvation of our own world.

2081
01:34:11,733 --> 01:34:13,100
No, gentlemen,

2082
01:34:13,700 --> 01:34:16,600
the flight of the RXM
was not a failure.

2083
01:34:17,000 --> 01:34:18,667
[Joel] But a resounding success.

2084
01:34:18,667 --> 01:34:21,267
Tomorrow we start construction
of RXM2.

2085
01:34:21,267 --> 01:34:24,000
[Joel] Oh, no, not again.

2086
01:34:25,567 --> 01:34:27,367
Let's get out of here.

2087
01:34:28,500 --> 01:34:30,567
-[Tom] Oh, look.
-[Joel] A rotten day.

2088
01:34:30,567 --> 01:34:32,300
Somebody stole my Woody.

2089
01:34:32,300 --> 01:34:35,533
[Crow] Oh, man.
What a crappy day.

2090
01:34:56,500 --> 01:35:00,100
Boy. Nothing more depressing
than being locked in a capsule

2091
01:35:00,100 --> 01:35:03,467
watching a movie about
people dying in a capsule.

2092
01:35:03,767 --> 01:35:06,767
Yeah, why don't
you just show us Marooned?

2093
01:35:06,767 --> 01:35:08,033
We couldn't get it.

2094
01:35:08,033 --> 01:35:11,233
Well, you wouldn't show terror
at 30,000 feet in an airplane,

2095
01:35:11,233 --> 01:35:12,400
would you?

2096
01:35:12,400 --> 01:35:13,633
[both] No.

2097
01:35:13,633 --> 01:35:16,767
Yeah, and you wouldn't show
Das Boot on a submarine,

2098
01:35:16,767 --> 01:35:17,967
would you?

2099
01:35:18,467 --> 01:35:20,433
Yeah, and you wouldn't show
a Burt Reynolds movie

2100
01:35:20,433 --> 01:35:22,333
to prisoners, would you?

2101
01:35:22,333 --> 01:35:24,133
Yeah, and you wouldn't show
Breakfast at Tiffany's

2102
01:35:24,133 --> 01:35:25,567
at a jewelry store.

2103
01:35:25,567 --> 01:35:27,033
-[all] Huh?
-There!

2104
01:35:27,033 --> 01:35:29,500
Oh, owie, owie, owie, owie,
owie, owie, owie, owie!

2105
01:35:29,500 --> 01:35:33,033
Don't you have some mail
to read, frog blossom?

2106
01:35:33,033 --> 01:35:34,100
Frog blossom?

2107
01:35:34,100 --> 01:35:36,500
Geez, just say so.
They're right here.

2108
01:35:37,167 --> 01:35:40,367
Cam-bot, let's put this one up
on the screen. It's visual.

2109
01:35:40,367 --> 01:35:43,600
Dear Joel, let me start off
by saying I love your show.

2110
01:35:43,600 --> 01:35:46,200
But I think it is time
to get some new robots.

2111
01:35:46,200 --> 01:35:47,267
[Crow, Tom] Hey.

2112
01:35:47,267 --> 01:35:50,933
[Joel] To aid you
and your guests in your quest

2113
01:35:50,933 --> 01:35:53,633
to find the dumbest
sci-fi movie ever made.

2114
01:35:53,633 --> 01:35:58,400
Sure Cam-bot, Tom, Gypsy,
Tom Servo and Crow are great,

2115
01:35:58,400 --> 01:36:00,467
but you need more guys
to fill up the empty seats

2116
01:36:00,467 --> 01:36:01,700
in front of the theater.

2117
01:36:01,700 --> 01:36:03,033
[Tom] Oh, I don't think so.

2118
01:36:03,033 --> 01:36:04,367
Well, I guess that's it.

2119
01:36:04,367 --> 01:36:06,333
Looks like you can bring us
down, Frank.

2120
01:36:06,333 --> 01:36:09,000
-Okay, sure.
-No, you idiot!

2121
01:36:09,000 --> 01:36:11,267
Tell them what you thought
about the experiment.

2122
01:36:11,267 --> 01:36:14,933
Uh, uh, that'll be $5.50.
Pull ahead, please.

2123
01:36:14,933 --> 01:36:16,500
Oh, never mind.

2124
01:36:16,700 --> 01:36:19,400
Until next time, Joely cakes.

2125
01:36:19,400 --> 01:36:21,033
All right, Frank,
push the button.

2126
01:36:23,100 --> 01:36:24,400
Oh, what did I do?

2127
01:36:24,667 --> 01:36:27,167
It's okay. Hit shift, clear.

2128
01:36:28,300 --> 01:36:30,000
Okay, now hit the button.

2129
01:36:31,833 --> 01:36:33,300
-This one?
-That one.

2130
01:36:33,300 --> 01:36:35,033
-This one?
-This one!

2131
01:36:36,467 --> 01:36:38,100
Sorry. He's new.

2132
01:36:39,400 --> 01:36:42,300
[theme music playing]

